var e={level:`B1`,type:`reading-mc`,questions:[{id:`b1-rm-001`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'every Saturday' and 'now she loves it'.`,passage:`Last month Emma started volunteering at an animal shelter. She goes there every Saturday and helps to feed the dogs and clean their cages. At first she was nervous because she had never worked with animals, but now she loves it. She is even thinking about studying to become a vet.`,items:[{q:`How often does Emma go to the shelter?`,options:[`Every day`,`Every Saturday`,`Once a month`],correct:1},{q:`How does she feel now?`,options:[`Nervous`,`Bored`,`Happy about the work`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-002`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'too far from the town centre' and 'a free bus now runs there'.`,passage:`The new shopping centre opened last week. It has over a hundred shops, a cinema and a large car park. Many people say that it is too far from the town centre, but a free bus now runs there every ten minutes.`,items:[{q:`What do some people complain about?`,options:[`The prices`,`The distance from the centre`,`The car park`],correct:1},{q:`How can people get there without a car?`,options:[`By a free bus`,`By train`,`On foot only`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-003`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says bees 'carry pollen' that helps plants produce fruit, numbers have 'fallen', and people with a balcony can grow lavender.`,passage:`Bees are very important for the food we eat. When they fly from flower to flower, they carry pollen, and this helps plants to produce fruit and seeds. However, the number of bees has fallen in many countries over the last thirty years. Scientists think there are several reasons for this, such as chemicals used on farms and the loss of wild flowers. The good news is that everyone can help. If you have a garden or even a small balcony, you can grow plants that bees like, for example lavender. You should also avoid using strong chemicals on your plants.`,items:[{q:`Why are bees important?`,options:[`They make plants grow taller.`,`They help plants to produce fruit and seeds.`,`They keep insects away from farms.`],correct:1},{q:`What has happened to bees in many countries?`,options:[`There are fewer of them.`,`They have moved to cities.`,`They have started eating different flowers.`],correct:0},{q:`What does the writer suggest people can do?`,options:[`Buy honey from local farms.`,`Use stronger chemicals.`,`Grow plants that bees like.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-004`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Marco 'used to spend every summer' at the farm; at first he 'missed his friends'; now he says the summers taught him patience and a love of nature.`,passage:`When Marco was twelve, he used to spend every summer at his grandfather's farm in the hills. There was no internet and no television, so he had to find other ways to pass the time. His grandfather taught him how to milk the cows, fix a fence and recognise different birds by their songs. At first Marco missed his friends and his computer games, but after a few weeks he started to enjoy the quiet life. Now Marco is thirty-five and works as a teacher in a big city. He says those summers taught him to be patient and to love nature.`,items:[{q:`Where did Marco spend his summers as a child?`,options:[`At the seaside`,`In a big city`,`On his grandfather's farm`],correct:2},{q:`How did Marco feel at the beginning?`,options:[`He missed his friends and games.`,`He was happy to have no internet.`,`He wanted to stay there forever.`],correct:0},{q:`What did those summers teach him?`,options:[`How to use a computer`,`To be patient and love nature`,`How to become a teacher`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-005`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Doctors say 'three or four cups a day' are safe; caffeine is 'also in tea, chocolate'; experts suggest stopping 'after two o'clock'.`,passage:`Many people drink coffee every morning because it helps them feel awake. Coffee contains caffeine, which affects the brain and makes us feel less tired. Doctors say that three or four cups a day are usually safe for healthy adults. However, drinking too much can cause problems. Some people feel nervous or have trouble sleeping, especially if they drink coffee late in the afternoon. Caffeine is not only found in coffee. It is also in tea, chocolate and many energy drinks. If you want to sleep better, experts suggest that you should stop drinking anything with caffeine after two o'clock.`,items:[{q:`According to doctors, how much coffee is usually safe for healthy adults?`,options:[`One cup a day`,`Three or four cups a day`,`As much as you like`],correct:1},{q:`Which of these also contains caffeine?`,options:[`Chocolate`,`Milk`,`Orange juice`],correct:0},{q:`What do experts suggest for better sleep?`,options:[`Drinking coffee only at night`,`Drinking tea instead of coffee`,`Avoiding caffeine after two o'clock`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-006`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Shop owners 'were worried that they would lose customers', but the survey shows more people now walk and stop at shops; the council plans to extend the project.`,passage:`Last year the town of Westbridge started a new project to reduce traffic in the centre. Cars are no longer allowed in the main square, and the council has built twenty kilometres of new bike lanes. People can also rent bicycles from stations around the town by using an app on their phones. At first many shop owners were worried that they would lose customers. However, a recent survey shows that more people now walk around the centre and stop at shops and cafés. The air is cleaner too, and the council is planning to extend the project next spring.`,items:[{q:`What can people use to rent a bicycle?`,options:[`A card from the council`,`An app on their phones`,`A ticket from the bus station`],correct:1},{q:`Why were shop owners worried at first?`,options:[`They thought they would lose customers.`,`They did not like bicycles.`,`They had to pay a new tax.`],correct:0},{q:`What is the council going to do next spring?`,options:[`Close the bike lanes`,`Allow cars in the square again`,`Extend the project`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-007`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Sunlight is 'made of many colours'; blue light 'is scattered' more; at sunset most blue light 'is lost on the way', so the sky looks orange or red.`,passage:`Have you ever wondered why the sky is blue? Sunlight looks white, but it is actually made of many colours. When sunlight enters the air around the Earth, it hits tiny pieces of gas. Blue light is scattered, or thrown in all directions, much more than red or yellow light. That is why we see blue everywhere when we look up during the day. At sunset, the light has to travel through much more air before it reaches our eyes. Most of the blue light is lost on the way, so the sky often looks orange or red.`,items:[{q:`What is sunlight made of?`,options:[`Only blue light`,`Many different colours`,`Tiny pieces of gas`],correct:1},{q:`Why do we see a blue sky during the day?`,options:[`Blue light is scattered more than other colours.`,`The sea reflects its colour into the sky.`,`The sun is blue in the middle of the day.`],correct:0},{q:`Why does the sky often look orange or red at sunset?`,options:[`The sun is colder in the evening.`,`There are more clouds at sunset.`,`Most of the blue light is lost on the way.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-008`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The days had 'only about five hours of light'; on her third night she saw green lights; she is 'planning to go back in summer'.`,passage:`Priya had always wanted to visit Iceland, so last winter she finally booked a trip. She stayed in a small guesthouse near a lake, two hours from the capital. The weather was much colder than she had expected, and the days were very short, with only about five hours of light. However, she was not disappointed. On her third night, the sky suddenly filled with green lights that moved like waves. The owner of the guesthouse told her that she was lucky, because some visitors stay for two weeks and never see them. Priya is already planning to go back in summer.`,items:[{q:`What surprised Priya about the weather?`,options:[`It was warmer than she had expected.`,`It rained every day.`,`It was much colder than she had expected.`],correct:2},{q:`What did she see on her third night?`,options:[`Green lights moving in the sky`,`A big storm over the lake`,`Snow falling on the capital`],correct:0},{q:`What is Priya planning to do?`,options:[`Move to Iceland`,`Return to Iceland in summer`,`Open a guesthouse`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-009`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The train could do the journey 'in less than two hours'; 'some doctors even said that high speeds were bad for the body'; towns near the lines 'grew quickly'.`,passage:`The first passenger railway in the world opened in England in 1830. It connected the cities of Liverpool and Manchester, about fifty kilometres apart. Before the railway, the journey took several hours by horse and carriage, but the train could do it in less than two hours. Many people were afraid of travelling so fast, and some doctors even said that high speeds were bad for the body. In spite of this, the railway was a great success. Within twenty years, trains were carrying millions of passengers across the country, and towns near the new lines grew quickly.`,items:[{q:`How long did the train journey take?`,options:[`Less than two hours`,`About five hours`,`A whole day`],correct:0},{q:`What did some doctors believe?`,options:[`Trains were good for people's health.`,`High speeds were bad for the body.`,`Horses were more dangerous than trains.`],correct:1},{q:`What happened to towns near the new railway lines?`,options:[`They became smaller.`,`They lost their shops.`,`They grew quickly.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-010`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`At first he could run only 'for five minutes'; an app told him 'when to run and when to walk'; he says the most important thing is 'going out regularly'.`,passage:`Sam started running two years ago because he wanted to get fit. In the beginning he could only run for five minutes before he had to stop. He downloaded an app that told him when to run and when to walk, and slowly he improved. After three months he could run for thirty minutes without stopping. Last month he took part in his first half marathon. He did not finish in a fast time, but he was very proud. Sam says the most important thing is not speed. It is going out regularly, even when you do not feel like it.`,items:[{q:`How long could Sam run when he started?`,options:[`Thirty minutes`,`Five minutes`,`One hour`],correct:1},{q:`How did the app help him?`,options:[`It told him when to run and when to walk.`,`It found him a running club.`,`It planned his meals.`],correct:0},{q:`According to Sam, what is the most important thing?`,options:[`Running very fast`,`Buying good shoes`,`Going out regularly`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-011`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`They change colour 'to hide from danger'; most nerve cells 'are actually in its arms'; most octopuses 'live for only one or two years'.`,passage:`Octopuses are some of the cleverest animals in the sea. They have eight arms and can change the colour of their skin in less than a second to hide from danger. Scientists have watched octopuses open jars to get food inside, and some have even escaped from their tanks in aquariums at night. An octopus has a very large brain for its size, but most of its nerve cells are actually in its arms. This means that each arm can feel and react on its own. Sadly, most octopuses live for only one or two years.`,items:[{q:`Why do octopuses change the colour of their skin?`,options:[`To find a partner`,`To hide from danger`,`To keep warm`],correct:1},{q:`Where are most of an octopus's nerve cells?`,options:[`In its brain`,`In its skin`,`In its arms`],correct:2},{q:`How long do most octopuses live?`,options:[`One or two years`,`About ten years`,`More than fifty years`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-012`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The 'biggest advantage' is not travelling to the office; some find it hard to stop working because their office is at home; experts recommend 'a fixed time to finish work'.`,passage:`More and more people are working from home at least two days a week. For many workers, the biggest advantage is that they do not have to travel to the office, so they save time and money. They also say they can concentrate better without colleagues talking around them. However, working from home is not perfect. Some people feel lonely, and others find it hard to stop working in the evening because their office is in the kitchen or the bedroom. Experts recommend having a fixed time to finish work and going for a short walk at lunchtime.`,items:[{q:`What is the biggest advantage for many home workers?`,options:[`They earn more money.`,`They do not have to travel to the office.`,`They can work with their friends.`],correct:1},{q:`Why do some people find it hard to stop working?`,options:[`Their office is in the kitchen or bedroom.`,`Their boss calls them every evening.`,`They have too many meetings.`],correct:0},{q:`What do experts recommend?`,options:[`Working late every day`,`Moving to a bigger house`,`Having a fixed time to finish work`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-013`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`She went to 'free language classes at the local library'; the chef 'started teaching her how to cook'; she gives jobs to newcomers because she 'remembers how difficult her first year was'.`,passage:`Ana moved from Brazil to Canada when she was twenty-two. She did not speak much English, and the winter was a shock because she had never seen snow before. She found a job washing dishes in a restaurant, and in the evenings she went to free language classes at the local library. The chef noticed that she was hard-working and started teaching her how to cook. Ten years later, Ana has opened her own small restaurant, where she serves Brazilian food. She often gives jobs to newcomers because she remembers how difficult her first year was.`,items:[{q:`Where did Ana learn English?`,options:[`At a university`,`At free classes in the library`,`From her colleagues at work`],correct:1},{q:`Who helped her learn to cook?`,options:[`Her mother`,`A teacher at the library`,`The chef at the restaurant`],correct:2},{q:`Why does Ana often give jobs to newcomers?`,options:[`She remembers how hard her first year was.`,`They accept lower pay.`,`The government tells her to do it.`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-014`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`We 'blink less often', so eyes become dry; the rule says look away 'every twenty minutes'; keep the phone 'at least thirty centimetres' from your face.`,passage:`Smartphones have made our lives easier in many ways, but they can also be bad for our eyes. When we look at a screen for a long time, we blink less often, so our eyes become dry and tired. Some people also get headaches. Eye doctors suggest a simple rule called the 20-20-20 rule. Every twenty minutes, you should look at something about six metres away for twenty seconds. This gives your eyes a short rest. It is also a good idea to keep your phone at least thirty centimetres from your face and to turn down the brightness at night.`,items:[{q:`Why do our eyes become dry when we look at a screen?`,options:[`We blink less often.`,`The screen is too hot.`,`We sit too far away.`],correct:0},{q:`How often should you rest your eyes, according to the rule?`,options:[`Every two hours`,`Every six minutes`,`Every twenty minutes`],correct:2},{q:`How far from your face should you keep your phone?`,options:[`Ten centimetres`,`At least thirty centimetres`,`Six metres`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-015`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Monarchs fly 'to the mountains of central Mexico'; 'no single butterfly makes the whole round trip'; 'Nobody is completely sure' how young ones know the way.`,passage:`Every autumn, millions of monarch butterflies fly from Canada and the north of the United States to the mountains of central Mexico. Some of them travel more than four thousand kilometres. Scientists were surprised to learn that no single butterfly makes the whole round trip. The butterflies that fly south in autumn spend the winter in Mexico and start the journey north in spring. However, they die on the way, and it is their children and grandchildren that finally arrive back in Canada. Nobody is completely sure how the young butterflies know where to go.`,items:[{q:`Where do the butterflies spend the winter?`,options:[`In Canada`,`In central Mexico`,`In the north of the United States`],correct:1},{q:`What surprised scientists?`,options:[`No single butterfly makes the whole round trip.`,`The butterflies travel only at night.`,`The butterflies never stop to eat.`],correct:0},{q:`What is still not completely clear?`,options:[`Why the butterflies are orange`,`How many butterflies there are`,`How the young butterflies know where to go`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-016`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`He noticed mould 'when he came back' from holiday; around the mould 'the bacteria had died'; production in large amounts 'took more than ten years'.`,passage:`In 1928, a Scottish scientist called Alexander Fleming went on holiday and left some dishes of bacteria in his laboratory. When he came back, he noticed that a type of mould had grown on one of the dishes. Around the mould, the bacteria had died. Fleming realised that the mould was producing something that could kill bacteria. He called it penicillin. However, it took more than ten years before other scientists found a way to produce it in large amounts. Penicillin was finally used to treat soldiers during the Second World War and saved thousands of lives.`,items:[{q:`When did Fleming notice the mould?`,options:[`After he returned from holiday`,`While he was teaching a class`,`During the Second World War`],correct:0},{q:`What had happened to the bacteria around the mould?`,options:[`They had grown faster.`,`They had changed colour.`,`They had died.`],correct:2},{q:`What took more than ten years?`,options:[`Finding the mould again`,`Producing penicillin in large amounts`,`Giving the medicine a name`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-017`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Their big bags were sent 'ahead by taxi'; Tom 'slipped on a muddy path'; they 'finished the walk one day late'.`,passage:`Lucy and her brother Tom decided to spend their holiday walking along the coast of Wales. They planned to walk about fifteen kilometres a day and sleep in small hotels on the way. They sent their big bags ahead by taxi, so they only had to carry water and a light lunch. On the second day it rained heavily all afternoon, and Tom slipped on a muddy path and hurt his ankle. They had to stop for a day while he rested. In the end they finished the walk one day late, but they both agreed it was the best holiday they had ever had.`,items:[{q:`How did their big bags travel?`,options:[`By taxi`,`By train`,`On their backs`],correct:0},{q:`What happened on the second day?`,options:[`They got lost in the hills.`,`Tom hurt his ankle.`,`Lucy lost her lunch.`],correct:1},{q:`How did the walk end?`,options:[`They gave up and went home.`,`They finished early.`,`They finished one day late.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-018`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Robots carrying things give nurses 'more time to spend with patients'; patients recover quickly 'because the cuts are smaller'; robots are 'very expensive'.`,passage:`Robots are already common in factories, but now they are starting to appear in hospitals too. In some hospitals, small robots carry medicine, food and clean sheets along the corridors, so nurses have more time to spend with patients. Surgeons also use robotic arms during some operations. The surgeon controls the arms from a screen, and the robot can make very small, exact movements. Patients often recover more quickly because the cuts are smaller. However, these robots are very expensive, so only large hospitals can usually afford them at the moment.`,items:[{q:`How do the small robots help nurses?`,options:[`They give nurses more time with patients.`,`They teach nurses new skills.`,`They do the operations for them.`],correct:0},{q:`Why do patients often recover more quickly after robotic operations?`,options:[`They stay in bed longer.`,`They take stronger medicine.`,`The cuts are smaller.`],correct:2},{q:`Why do only large hospitals usually have these robots?`,options:[`Small hospitals do not need them.`,`The robots are very expensive.`,`Doctors in small hospitals do not like them.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-019`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The brain 'stores what we have learned'; teenagers 'need even more' than seven to nine hours; one of the best ways to sleep well is 'going to bed at the same time every night'.`,passage:`Sleep is just as important for our health as food and exercise. While we are sleeping, the body repairs itself and the brain stores what we have learned during the day. Most adults need between seven and nine hours a night, while teenagers need even more. Unfortunately, many people do not get enough sleep because they stay up late watching series or checking their phones. If this happens often, they may find it difficult to concentrate and may get ill more easily. Going to bed at the same time every night is one of the best ways to sleep well.`,items:[{q:`What does the brain do while we sleep?`,options:[`It stops working completely.`,`It stores what we have learned.`,`It makes us hungry.`],correct:1},{q:`Who needs the most sleep, according to the text?`,options:[`Teenagers`,`Adults`,`Older people`],correct:0},{q:`What is one of the best ways to sleep well?`,options:[`Watching a series in bed`,`Sleeping late at weekends`,`Going to bed at the same time every night`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-020`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`A boat comes 'only once a week'; the light 'is automatic' today; he feels lonely 'sometimes in winter' but 'would never change his job'.`,passage:`Kenji has worked as a lighthouse keeper on a small island for eighteen years. The island is two hours by boat from the mainland, and a boat brings food and letters only once a week. In the past, his main job was to make sure the light worked every night. Today the light is automatic, so Kenji spends most of his time checking the machines, recording the weather and helping fishermen when there is a problem. People often ask him if he feels lonely. He says he sometimes does in winter, but he would never change his job.`,items:[{q:`How often does a boat come to the island?`,options:[`Every day`,`Twice a month`,`Once a week`],correct:2},{q:`Why does Kenji no longer need to turn on the light?`,options:[`The light is automatic now.`,`Ships do not use the light any more.`,`A fisherman does it for him.`],correct:0},{q:`How does Kenji feel about his job?`,options:[`He wants to find a new job soon.`,`He would never change it.`,`He only likes it in winter.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-021`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`It is 'a series of walls'; it was made 'to protect the country from attacks from the north'; the story about seeing it from space 'is not true'.`,passage:`The Great Wall of China is not one single wall. It is actually a series of walls that were built and rebuilt over about two thousand years by different rulers. Most of the parts that tourists visit today were built during the Ming dynasty, between the fourteenth and seventeenth centuries. The wall was made to protect the country from attacks from the north. A popular story says that you can see it from space without any special equipment, but this is not true. The wall is very long, but it is quite narrow and the same colour as the land around it.`,items:[{q:`What is the Great Wall actually?`,options:[`A single wall built by one ruler`,`A series of walls built over many years`,`A road between two cities`],correct:1},{q:`Why was the wall built?`,options:[`To mark the end of a river`,`To attract visitors`,`To protect the country from attacks`],correct:2},{q:`What does the writer say about seeing the wall from space?`,options:[`It is a popular story, but it is not true.`,`It is easy on a clear day.`,`Astronauts see it every day.`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-022`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Many volcanoes are 'around the Pacific Ocean'; people live near them because 'volcanic soil is very rich'; scientists measure 'small earthquakes and changes in the gases'.`,passage:`Volcanoes are openings in the Earth's surface where hot melted rock, gas and ash come out. There are about one thousand five hundred active volcanoes in the world, and many of them are around the Pacific Ocean. Although eruptions can be very dangerous, millions of people live near volcanoes. One reason is that volcanic soil is very rich, so farmers can grow excellent crops. Today scientists watch active volcanoes carefully. They measure small earthquakes and changes in the gases, and this often helps them to warn people before a big eruption happens.`,items:[{q:`Where are many active volcanoes found?`,options:[`In the middle of deserts`,`Near the North Pole`,`Around the Pacific Ocean`],correct:2},{q:`Why do many people live near volcanoes?`,options:[`The soil is very good for farming.`,`The weather is always warm.`,`Houses there are cheaper.`],correct:0},{q:`How do scientists try to warn people about eruptions?`,options:[`By looking at old maps`,`By measuring small earthquakes and gases`,`By asking local farmers`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-023`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Her parents 'could not afford lessons'; she has a lesson 'every Thursday'; she was 'the oldest player by fifty years' and got 'the loudest applause'.`,passage:`When Grace retired from her job as a nurse at sixty-five, her family expected her to relax and spend more time in the garden. Instead, she decided to learn something completely new: the piano. She had always loved music, but her parents could not afford lessons when she was a child. Now she practises for an hour every morning and has a lesson with a young teacher every Thursday. Last December she played two short pieces at a concert for her teacher's students. She was the oldest player by fifty years, and she received the loudest applause.`,items:[{q:`Why didn't Grace learn the piano as a child?`,options:[`She did not like music.`,`Her parents could not pay for lessons.`,`She was too busy at school.`],correct:1},{q:`How often does she have a lesson?`,options:[`Once a week`,`Every morning`,`Once a month`],correct:0},{q:`What happened at the concert?`,options:[`She forgot the music.`,`She played with her family.`,`She received the loudest applause.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-024`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Turtles eat bags 'because they look like food'; the law said shops 'had to charge customers'; people used 'over ninety per cent fewer bags'.`,passage:`Plastic bags are cheap and useful, but they are a serious problem for the environment. Most of them are used for only a few minutes, yet they can take hundreds of years to break down. Many end up in rivers and seas, where animals such as turtles eat them because they look like food. In 2015, a law in England said that shops had to charge customers for plastic bags. Within a few years, people were using over ninety per cent fewer bags from large supermarkets. Many shoppers now carry their own cloth bags when they go shopping.`,items:[{q:`Why do turtles eat plastic bags?`,options:[`They are very hungry in winter.`,`The bags look like food.`,`The bags taste sweet.`],correct:1},{q:`What did the law in England say?`,options:[`Shops had to charge for plastic bags.`,`Plastic bags were not allowed in shops.`,`Shops had to give free cloth bags.`],correct:0},{q:`What was the result of the law?`,options:[`People used more bags.`,`Supermarkets closed.`,`People used far fewer bags.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-025`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The two computers were 'next to each other in the same room'; the message was probably 'a group of letters to test the system'; he 'chose the @ symbol'.`,passage:`The first e-mail was sent in 1971 by an American engineer called Ray Tomlinson. He sent it between two computers that were standing next to each other in the same room. He later said that he could not remember what the message was, but it was probably just a group of letters to test the system. Tomlinson was also the person who chose the @ symbol to separate the name of the user from the name of the computer. Today, more than three hundred billion e-mails are sent every day, although many of them are advertisements.`,items:[{q:`Where were the two computers?`,options:[`In different countries`,`In different cities`,`In the same room`],correct:2},{q:`What was the first message probably like?`,options:[`A long letter to his boss`,`A test with a group of letters`,`A birthday message`],correct:1},{q:`What else did Tomlinson do?`,options:[`He chose the @ symbol for e-mail addresses.`,`He built the first computer.`,`He invented advertisements.`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-026`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Safaris are early or late because animals are 'most active'; Daniel likes elephants because they are 'very intelligent'; a tourist who ignored the rule 'was chased by a buffalo'.`,passage:`Daniel works as a guide in a national park in Kenya. He takes small groups of tourists on safaris in an open jeep, usually early in the morning or late in the afternoon, when the animals are most active. He knows the park very well because he grew up in a village nearby. His favourite animals are elephants, which he says are very intelligent and care for each other like a family. Daniel always tells visitors to stay quiet and never to get out of the jeep. Last year, a tourist who ignored this rule was chased by a buffalo.`,items:[{q:`Why does Daniel take tourists out early or late in the day?`,options:[`The animals are most active then.`,`It is cheaper for tourists.`,`The park is closed at midday.`],correct:0},{q:`Why does Daniel like elephants?`,options:[`They are the largest animals.`,`They are easy to find.`,`They are intelligent and care for each other.`],correct:2},{q:`What happened to the tourist who ignored Daniel's rule?`,options:[`He got lost in the park.`,`A buffalo chased him.`,`He had to pay a fine.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-027`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Laughing produces chemicals that 'reduce pain'; people who watched a funny film 'felt more relaxed'; we laugh 'thirty times more often' with other people.`,passage:`Laughing is good for you, and scientists have found several reasons why. When we laugh, our body produces chemicals that make us feel happier and reduce pain. Laughing also lowers stress and may help our heart, because it makes blood move more easily around the body. In one study, people who watched a funny film for fifteen minutes felt more relaxed than people who watched a serious documentary. Interestingly, we laugh about thirty times more often when we are with other people than when we are alone. So spending time with friends is good for your health too.`,items:[{q:`What do the chemicals produced when we laugh do?`,options:[`They make us hungry.`,`They make us feel happier and reduce pain.`,`They make us sleep.`],correct:1},{q:`How did people feel after watching a funny film?`,options:[`More relaxed`,`More tired`,`More worried`],correct:0},{q:`When do we laugh most?`,options:[`When we are alone`,`When we watch documentaries`,`When we are with other people`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-028`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`There are 'no cars at all' in the historic centre; floods happen 'in late autumn and winter'; the city 'charges day visitors a small entry fee'.`,passage:`Venice in Italy is one of the most popular tourist cities in the world. It is built on more than one hundred small islands, and instead of roads it has canals. People travel by boat, and there are no cars at all in the historic centre. However, Venice has some serious problems. The city floods several times a year, especially in late autumn and winter, when high tides push water into the streets. In addition, millions of tourists visit every year, while the number of local people living there has fallen a lot. The city now charges day visitors a small entry fee on busy days.`,items:[{q:`How do people travel in the historic centre of Venice?`,options:[`By car`,`By bus`,`By boat`],correct:2},{q:`When does the city usually flood?`,options:[`In late autumn and winter`,`Only in summer`,`Every day`],correct:0},{q:`What has the city done about the large number of tourists?`,options:[`It has closed the canals.`,`It charges day visitors a small fee on busy days.`,`It has built new hotels.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-029`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`She moved to Paris because women 'were not allowed to study at university' in Poland; she won Nobel Prizes in 1903 and 1911; her work 'damaged her health'.`,passage:`Marie Curie was born in Poland in 1867. At that time, women were not allowed to study at university in her country, so she moved to Paris, where she studied physics and mathematics. She was often cold and hungry because she had very little money. Together with her husband Pierre, she discovered two new elements, polonium and radium. In 1903 she became the first woman to win a Nobel Prize, and in 1911 she won a second one. Sadly, her work with radioactive materials damaged her health, because people did not yet understand how dangerous they were.`,items:[{q:`Why did Marie Curie move to Paris?`,options:[`She could study at university there.`,`Her husband lived there.`,`She wanted to find a job in a hospital.`],correct:0},{q:`How many Nobel Prizes did she win?`,options:[`One`,`Two`,`Three`],correct:1},{q:`Why did her work damage her health?`,options:[`She worked too many hours.`,`The laboratory was cold.`,`People did not know radioactive materials were dangerous.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-030`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`It is useful for people 'who live far from big shops'; clothes are sent back because 'they are the wrong size'; small shops 'cannot compete with the low prices online'.`,passage:`Online shopping has changed the way many people buy things. You can order almost anything with a few clicks, and it often arrives the next day. It is especially useful for people who live far from big shops or who cannot easily leave the house. However, there are some disadvantages. You cannot touch or try on the products, so many clothes are sent back because they are the wrong size. Each return means another journey for a delivery van. Some small shops in town centres have also closed because they cannot compete with the low prices online.`,items:[{q:`Who finds online shopping especially useful?`,options:[`People who live far from big shops`,`People who work in shops`,`People who like delivery vans`],correct:0},{q:`Why are many clothes sent back?`,options:[`They arrive too late.`,`They are too expensive.`,`They are the wrong size.`],correct:2},{q:`Why have some small shops closed?`,options:[`Their rent is too high.`,`They cannot compete with online prices.`,`Nobody goes into town centres.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-031`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`She lived on 'sandwiches and instant noodles'; the rice burned 'while she was talking on the phone'; her flatmates 'paid for the ingredients'.`,passage:`Tara had never cooked a proper meal before she went to university. Her parents always did the cooking at home, so in her first weeks she lived on sandwiches and instant noodles. She soon felt tired all the time and decided she had to change. She started watching short cooking videos and tried one new recipe every weekend. Some meals were a disaster, like the rice that burned while she was talking on the phone. But slowly she got better. By the end of the year, she was cooking dinner for her flatmates every Friday, and they paid for the ingredients.`,items:[{q:`What did Tara eat in her first weeks?`,options:[`Meals her parents sent`,`Sandwiches and instant noodles`,`Food from the university café`],correct:1},{q:`Why did the rice burn?`,options:[`She was talking on the phone.`,`She was watching a video.`,`She was asleep.`],correct:0},{q:`What did her flatmates do in return for dinner?`,options:[`They washed the dishes.`,`They taught her new recipes.`,`They paid for the ingredients.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-032`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`At sea 'the wind is stronger and more regular'; wind energy is clean because it 'does not produce harmful gases'; some people say turbines 'spoil the view'.`,passage:`Wind farms are groups of large turbines that turn the energy of the wind into electricity. Some are built on hills, but more and more are now built at sea, where the wind is stronger and more regular. One large turbine can produce enough electricity for thousands of homes. Wind energy is clean, because it does not produce harmful gases like coal or oil. However, not everyone is happy about wind farms. Some people think the turbines spoil the view, and others are worried about birds that may fly into them. Engineers are now designing turbines that are safer for birds.`,items:[{q:`Why are more wind farms built at sea?`,options:[`The land is too expensive.`,`The wind is stronger and more regular.`,`Fewer birds live at sea.`],correct:1},{q:`Why is wind energy called clean?`,options:[`It does not produce harmful gases.`,`It is very cheap.`,`The turbines are washed by the rain.`],correct:0},{q:`What is one complaint about wind farms?`,options:[`They are too noisy at night.`,`They need a lot of water.`,`They spoil the view.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-033`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`It hit an iceberg 'on the fourth night'; many died 'because there were not enough lifeboats'; new rules said ships 'must carry enough lifeboats'.`,passage:`When the Titanic left Southampton in April 1912, people said it was the safest ship ever built. It was the largest ship in the world at that time and carried more than two thousand passengers and crew. On the fourth night of its first journey, while it was crossing the Atlantic, it hit an iceberg. The ship sank in less than three hours. More than one thousand five hundred people died, partly because there were not enough lifeboats for everyone. After the disaster, new rules said that every ship must carry enough lifeboats for all the people on board.`,items:[{q:`When did the Titanic hit the iceberg?`,options:[`On the first night`,`On the fourth night`,`When it arrived in America`],correct:1},{q:`Why did so many people die?`,options:[`The crew did not see the iceberg.`,`The sea was too warm.`,`There were not enough lifeboats.`],correct:2},{q:`What changed after the disaster?`,options:[`Ships had to carry enough lifeboats for everyone.`,`Ships could not cross the Atlantic.`,`Ships had to be smaller.`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-034`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Fires are 'only a small part of his work'; he works 'in shifts'; the hardest part is 'seeing people who have lost their homes'.`,passage:`Ahmed is a firefighter in a busy city. People think that his job is mostly about putting out fires, but in fact fires are only a small part of his work. He and his team also help at car accidents, rescue people who are stuck in lifts and visit schools to teach children about safety at home. Ahmed works in shifts, so some weeks he works at night and sleeps during the day. He says the hardest part of the job is not the danger but seeing people who have lost their homes. The best part, he says, is the team.`,items:[{q:`What does Ahmed say about fires?`,options:[`They are only a small part of his work.`,`They are the only thing he does.`,`They happen mostly in schools.`],correct:0},{q:`Why does Ahmed sometimes sleep during the day?`,options:[`He is often ill.`,`He works in shifts.`,`He has a second job.`],correct:1},{q:`What is the hardest part of the job for Ahmed?`,options:[`The danger`,`Working at night`,`Seeing people who have lost their homes`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-035`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Some penguins live 'in warmer places, such as South Africa'; fat and feathers 'keep them warm and dry'; emperor penguins 'take turns standing on the outside'.`,passage:`Penguins are birds, but they cannot fly. Instead, they are excellent swimmers and use their wings like flippers to move quickly through the water. Most people imagine penguins living on ice, but some kinds live in warmer places, such as South Africa and the Galápagos Islands. Penguins have a thick layer of fat and tightly packed feathers that keep them warm and dry. In the coldest parts of Antarctica, emperor penguins stand close together in large groups during winter storms. They take turns standing on the outside of the group, so that no bird gets too cold.`,items:[{q:`Where do some kinds of penguins live?`,options:[`In warm places such as South Africa`,`In forests in Europe`,`Only in Antarctica`],correct:0},{q:`What keeps penguins warm and dry?`,options:[`Their long legs`,`Building nests of grass`,`A layer of fat and tightly packed feathers`],correct:2},{q:`How do emperor penguins survive winter storms?`,options:[`They fly to warmer countries.`,`They stand close together and take turns on the outside.`,`They hide under the ice.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-036`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Video calls by doctors are useful for 'people in the countryside'; one reason they are tiring is that 'we look at our own face'; experts suggest 'turning off your camera now and then'.`,passage:`Video calls have become a normal part of life for millions of people. Families use them to stay in touch with relatives who live abroad, and companies use them for meetings instead of paying for expensive business trips. Doctors in some countries also speak to patients by video, which is very useful for people in the countryside. However, many people say that long video meetings are more tiring than meetings in person. One reason is that we look at our own face on the screen for a long time. Experts suggest turning off your camera now and then.`,items:[{q:`Who finds video calls with doctors very useful?`,options:[`People in big cities`,`People in the countryside`,`Doctors who travel abroad`],correct:1},{q:`What is one reason video meetings are tiring?`,options:[`We look at our own face for a long time.`,`The internet is often slow.`,`Meetings start very early.`],correct:0},{q:`What do experts suggest?`,options:[`Having longer meetings`,`Using two screens`,`Turning off your camera now and then`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-037`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`As a child she 'nearly drowned'; for the first two lessons she 'only stood in the shallow end'; next summer she is going to 'swim in the sea with her children'.`,passage:`Hannah had a fear of water for most of her life. When she was a child, she fell into a swimming pool and nearly drowned, and after that she refused to go near the sea. At forty, she decided she was tired of sitting on the beach while her children played in the waves. She joined a class for adults who were afraid of water. For the first two lessons, she only stood in the shallow end. After six months, she swam a full length of the pool. Next summer, she is going to swim in the sea with her children.`,items:[{q:`Why was Hannah afraid of water?`,options:[`She nearly drowned as a child.`,`She saw a shark on holiday.`,`Her parents never let her swim.`],correct:0},{q:`What did she do in the first two lessons?`,options:[`She swam a full length.`,`She jumped into the deep end.`,`She only stood in the shallow end.`],correct:2},{q:`What is she planning to do next summer?`,options:[`Teach swimming to adults`,`Swim in the sea with her children`,`Stay on the beach`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-038`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The first line opened 'just before the Olympic Games'; a late train means passengers get 'a special note to show their employer'; cleaners prepare each train 'in only seven minutes'.`,passage:`Japan is famous for its fast trains, called Shinkansen, which are sometimes known as bullet trains in English. The first line opened in 1964, just before the Olympic Games in Tokyo. Today the trains can travel at up to three hundred and twenty kilometres an hour. They are also extremely punctual. On average, a train is less than one minute late. If a train is more than five minutes late, the company often gives passengers a special note to show their employer. The trains are very clean as well, because teams of cleaners prepare each one in only seven minutes.`,items:[{q:`When did the first Shinkansen line open?`,options:[`Just after the Second World War`,`In the year 2000`,`Just before the Olympic Games in Tokyo`],correct:2},{q:`What can passengers get if a train is more than five minutes late?`,options:[`A note to show their employer`,`Their money back`,`A free meal`],correct:0},{q:`How long do cleaners have to prepare each train?`,options:[`One minute`,`Seven minutes`,`Thirty minutes`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-039`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Children who eat breakfast 'concentrate better at school'; sugary cereal makes you 'feel hungry again by ten o'clock'; eggs and yoghurt 'keep you full for longer'.`,passage:`Many people think that eating breakfast is the most important part of a healthy day. Some studies show that children who eat breakfast concentrate better at school. However, other research suggests that for adults it is more important to think about what you eat, not when you eat. A breakfast of sugary cereal and white bread may give you energy quickly, but you will probably feel hungry again by ten o'clock. Food such as eggs, yoghurt, fruit and whole grains will keep you full for longer. If you are not hungry in the morning, you do not have to eat immediately.`,items:[{q:`What do some studies show about children who eat breakfast?`,options:[`They sleep better.`,`They concentrate better at school.`,`They grow taller.`],correct:1},{q:`What may happen after a breakfast of sugary cereal?`,options:[`You will feel hungry again quickly.`,`You will feel full all day.`,`You will not need lunch.`],correct:0},{q:`Which food will keep you full for longer?`,options:[`White bread`,`Sweet biscuits`,`Eggs and yoghurt`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-040`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`He learned by watching 'online videos'; he gives one bike every month 'to a local charity'; he hopes his hobby 'will become a real business'.`,passage:`Leo is a nineteen-year-old who repairs old bicycles in his garage. He started three years ago, when he found a broken bike next to a rubbish bin and decided to fix it. He watched online videos to learn how to change the brakes and the chain. Now he buys old bikes cheaply, repairs them and sells them to students. He also gives one bike every month to a local charity that helps refugees get to work. Leo is studying engineering at college, but he hopes that one day his hobby will become a real business with its own shop.`,items:[{q:`How did Leo learn to repair bikes?`,options:[`From his father`,`At a course in college`,`By watching online videos`],correct:2},{q:`What does he do every month?`,options:[`He gives a bike to a local charity.`,`He opens a new shop.`,`He buys a new bike for himself.`],correct:0},{q:`What does Leo hope for the future?`,options:[`To become a professional cyclist`,`To turn his hobby into a business with a shop`,`To stop studying engineering`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-041`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The Moon's gravity 'causes the tides'; its light is 'light from the Sun that is reflected'; the first people walked on the Moon 'in 1969'.`,passage:`The Moon has always affected life on Earth. Its gravity pulls on the oceans and causes the tides, so the level of the sea rises and falls twice a day. The Moon does not produce its own light. What we see is light from the Sun that is reflected by its surface. Because the Moon goes around the Earth about once a month, we see different parts of it lit by the Sun, and its shape seems to change. In 1969, two American astronauts became the first people to walk on the Moon. Their footprints are probably still there.`,items:[{q:`What causes the tides?`,options:[`The wind over the ocean`,`The Moon's gravity`,`Heat from the Sun`],correct:1},{q:`Where does the light of the Moon come from?`,options:[`The Moon produces it.`,`It comes from the stars.`,`It is light from the Sun.`],correct:2},{q:`What happened in 1969?`,options:[`Two astronauts walked on the Moon.`,`Scientists discovered the tides.`,`The Moon changed its shape.`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-042`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Writing was first used 'to keep records of business'; people pressed marks 'into wet clay'; tablets 'have survived', so we know about daily life.`,passage:`Before writing was invented, people passed on their history by telling stories. Writing first appeared in Mesopotamia, in what is now Iraq, more than five thousand years ago. At first, people used it to keep records of business, such as how much grain or how many animals someone owned. They pressed marks into wet clay with a pointed stick, and then they left the clay in the sun to dry. Later, people started using writing for laws, letters and poems. Many of these clay tablets have survived, so we know a lot about daily life at that time.`,items:[{q:`What was writing first used for?`,options:[`Writing poems`,`Keeping business records`,`Sending letters to friends`],correct:1},{q:`How did people write in Mesopotamia?`,options:[`They pressed marks into wet clay.`,`They painted on walls.`,`They wrote with ink on paper.`],correct:0},{q:`Why do we know a lot about daily life at that time?`,options:[`People told stories about it.`,`Kings wrote history books.`,`Many clay tablets have survived.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-043`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`She gets up 'at four o'clock'; some dough 'rests for twenty-four hours'; more people came because 'they preferred her bread'.`,passage:`Rosa runs a small bakery in a mountain village. She gets up at four o'clock every morning to start baking, and by seven the first customers are already waiting at the door. Her bread is made in the traditional way, with only flour, water, salt and time. Some of her dough rests for twenty-four hours before it goes into the oven. A few years ago, a large supermarket opened in the next town, and Rosa was afraid she would have to close. Instead, more people started coming, because they preferred her bread to the cheaper bread in the supermarket.`,items:[{q:`What time does Rosa start baking?`,options:[`At four o'clock`,`At seven o'clock`,`At midnight`],correct:0},{q:`What is special about some of her dough?`,options:[`It contains sugar and milk.`,`It is made by a machine.`,`It rests for twenty-four hours.`],correct:2},{q:`What happened after the supermarket opened?`,options:[`Rosa closed her bakery.`,`More people came to Rosa's bakery.`,`Rosa started selling cheaper bread.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-044`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Flying in a V means the birds 'use less energy'; the shape makes it easier 'to see the others'; a tired leader 'moves to the back'.`,passage:`Have you ever noticed that some birds fly in a V shape? Geese, for example, often do this when they travel long distances. Scientists believe there are two main reasons. First, each bird flies slightly behind and to the side of the bird in front, where the air helps to lift it. This means the birds use less energy and can fly further. Second, the V shape makes it easier for every bird to see the others. The bird at the front works the hardest, so the birds change places regularly, and a tired leader moves to the back.`,items:[{q:`What is one advantage of flying in a V shape?`,options:[`The birds can fly faster than planes.`,`The birds use less energy.`,`The birds can sleep while flying.`],correct:1},{q:`Which bird works the hardest?`,options:[`The bird at the back`,`The youngest bird`,`The bird at the front`],correct:2},{q:`What does a tired leader do?`,options:[`It moves to the back.`,`It lands and rests.`,`It leaves the group.`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-045`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Electric cars are 'cheaper to run' because electricity costs less; people worry 'the battery will run out'; governments 'are building more' charging points.`,passage:`Electric cars are becoming more popular in many countries. They do not produce gases from an exhaust pipe, so they help to keep the air in cities cleaner. They are also very quiet and cheaper to run than petrol cars, because electricity usually costs less than fuel. However, many drivers are still not sure about buying one. Electric cars are often more expensive to buy, and some people worry that the battery will run out on a long journey. In some areas there are still not many charging points, although governments are building more every year.`,items:[{q:`Why are electric cars cheaper to run?`,options:[`They never need repairs.`,`Electricity usually costs less than fuel.`,`They are smaller than petrol cars.`],correct:1},{q:`What worries some drivers?`,options:[`The battery may run out on a long journey.`,`The cars are too noisy.`,`The cars produce too much gas.`],correct:0},{q:`What are governments doing?`,options:[`Making petrol cheaper`,`Stopping the sale of electric cars`,`Building more charging points`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-046`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`They missed a bus 'because their first train was delayed'; their favourite part was 'a cooking class'; Mia 'will never plan a trip in such detail again'.`,passage:`Mia and her friend Jess spent three weeks travelling around Vietnam by train and bus. They had planned every day before they left, but they soon realised that the plan was too busy. On the fourth day they missed a bus because their first train was delayed, so they decided to be more flexible. They stayed longer in places they liked and skipped some cities on their list. Their favourite part was a cooking class in a small village, where a local family showed them how to make spring rolls. Mia says she will never plan a trip in such detail again.`,items:[{q:`Why did they miss a bus?`,options:[`They woke up late.`,`They went to the wrong station.`,`Their train was delayed.`],correct:2},{q:`What was their favourite part of the trip?`,options:[`A cooking class in a village`,`A visit to a big city`,`A boat trip on a river`],correct:0},{q:`What has Mia learned from the trip?`,options:[`She should plan every day carefully.`,`She should not plan trips in so much detail.`,`She should always travel by bus.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-047`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Not drinking enough can cause 'headaches'; the amount 'depends on your size, how active you are and the weather'; fruit, vegetables and soup 'also contain a lot of water'.`,passage:`Drinking enough water is very important for your health. About sixty per cent of the human body is water, and we lose some of it every day when we breathe, sweat and go to the toilet. If we do not drink enough, we may get headaches, feel tired or find it hard to concentrate. Many people believe that everyone must drink exactly two litres of water a day, but the amount you need depends on your size, how active you are and the weather. Remember that food such as fruit, vegetables and soup also contains a lot of water.`,items:[{q:`What can happen if you do not drink enough?`,options:[`You may get headaches.`,`You may sleep too much.`,`You may grow faster.`],correct:0},{q:`What does the writer say about drinking two litres a day?`,options:[`Everyone must do it.`,`It is too much for adults.`,`The amount you need depends on different things.`],correct:2},{q:`Which of these also gives the body water?`,options:[`Bread`,`Soup`,`Rice`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-048`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The flood left 'rich black mud' where farmers planted crops; the Egyptians made 'a calendar based on' the floods; the pyramids were 'tombs for their kings'.`,passage:`In ancient Egypt, the River Nile was the centre of life. Almost all of the country was desert, but every summer the river flooded and covered the land on both sides with rich black mud. When the water went down, farmers planted wheat and other crops in the mud. Because the floods were so important, the Egyptians created a calendar based on them. They also built huge stone pyramids as tombs for their kings, called pharaohs. The largest one, at Giza, was built about four and a half thousand years ago and was the tallest building in the world for almost four thousand years.`,items:[{q:`What did the summer floods leave on the land?`,options:[`Sand from the desert`,`Rich black mud`,`Stones for building`],correct:1},{q:`What did the Egyptians base their calendar on?`,options:[`The floods of the Nile`,`The movement of the stars`,`The birthdays of their kings`],correct:0},{q:`What were the pyramids?`,options:[`Temples for farmers`,`Places to store wheat`,`Tombs for the pharaohs`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-049`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`In London he 'hardly ever saw daylight in winter'; in Scotland they earn 'much less money'; the first winter was hard 'because the roof leaked'.`,passage:`Ben had worked in an office in London for ten years when he decided he needed a change. He was always stressed, and he hardly ever saw daylight in winter. He gave up his job, sold his flat and bought an old farmhouse in Scotland with his wife. Now they grow vegetables, keep chickens and rent two rooms to tourists in summer. They earn much less money than before, and the first winter was very hard because the roof leaked. But Ben says he has never felt healthier, and he does not miss his old life at all.`,items:[{q:`What was one problem with Ben's life in London?`,options:[`He hardly ever saw daylight in winter.`,`He lived too far from his office.`,`He did not earn enough money.`],correct:0},{q:`How is their income now compared to before?`,options:[`It is higher.`,`It is much lower.`,`It is the same.`],correct:1},{q:`Why was the first winter difficult?`,options:[`The chickens got ill.`,`No tourists came.`,`The roof leaked.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-050`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Corals 'are actually animals'; when the sea is 'too warm' they turn white; scientists are trying 'to grow stronger corals'.`,passage:`Coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, but they are home to about a quarter of all sea creatures. Corals may look like plants or rocks, but they are actually animals. They live together with tiny plants that give them food and their bright colours. When the sea becomes too warm, the corals push these plants out, turn white and may die. This is called coral bleaching, and it has become more common as the oceans get warmer. Scientists are now trying to grow stronger corals that can survive in warmer water.`,items:[{q:`What are corals?`,options:[`Plants`,`Animals`,`Rocks`],correct:1},{q:`What happens when the sea becomes too warm?`,options:[`The corals grow faster.`,`The corals move to deeper water.`,`The corals turn white and may die.`],correct:2},{q:`What are scientists trying to do?`,options:[`Grow corals that can survive warmer water`,`Move fish to new reefs`,`Paint the corals`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-051`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`People who try it say they 'sleep better'; many feel anxious because they are afraid of 'missing important messages'; experts suggest 'not using your phone during meals'.`,passage:`Most of us check our phones dozens of times a day, often without thinking about it. Some people have started taking a digital detox, which means spending a period of time without phones, computers or social media. This could be one evening a week or a whole weekend. People who have tried it say they sleep better, read more books and have longer conversations with their family. However, it is not easy at the beginning. Many feel anxious because they are afraid of missing important messages. Experts suggest starting small, for example by not using your phone during meals.`,items:[{q:`What do people who have tried a digital detox say?`,options:[`They sleep better.`,`They work longer hours.`,`They make more friends online.`],correct:0},{q:`Why do many people feel anxious at the beginning?`,options:[`They have nothing to do.`,`They are afraid of missing important messages.`,`They feel bored with their family.`],correct:1},{q:`How do experts suggest starting?`,options:[`By giving away your phone`,`By stopping for a whole month`,`By not using your phone during meals`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-052`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Lisbon is 'built on seven hills'; tram 28 is often 'crowded'; locals advise taking it 'early in the morning' and watching your bags because of 'pickpockets'.`,passage:`Lisbon, the capital of Portugal, is built on seven hills, so walking around the city can be tiring. Fortunately, there are old yellow trams that climb the steep streets, and some of them have been running for more than a hundred years. The most famous is tram number 28, which passes many of the city's main sights. It is very popular with tourists, so it is often crowded and you may have to wait a long time. Local people advise visitors to take it early in the morning, and to keep an eye on their bags, because pickpockets sometimes work on busy trams.`,items:[{q:`Why can walking around Lisbon be tiring?`,options:[`The city is very big.`,`It is always hot.`,`The city is built on hills.`],correct:2},{q:`What is the problem with tram number 28?`,options:[`It is often crowded.`,`It is very expensive.`,`It does not stop at the main sights.`],correct:0},{q:`Why should visitors keep an eye on their bags?`,options:[`The trams move very fast.`,`Pickpockets sometimes work on busy trams.`,`Bags are not allowed on trams.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-053`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The team carries 'a small solar panel to charge their equipment'; they set up a clinic 'in the school'; Elena 'also trains local health workers'.`,passage:`Elena is a doctor who works for a charity in remote parts of Nepal. Twice a year, she and a small team walk for several days to reach villages where there are no roads and no hospitals. They carry medicine, bandages and a small solar panel to charge their equipment. In each village they set up a clinic in the school and see up to a hundred patients a day. Many people have walked for hours to get there. Elena says the work is exhausting, but it is the most rewarding thing she has ever done. She also trains local health workers.`,items:[{q:`Why does the team carry a solar panel?`,options:[`To heat water`,`To charge their equipment`,`To cook food`],correct:1},{q:`Where do they set up their clinic?`,options:[`In a tent outside the village`,`In the village hospital`,`In the school`],correct:2},{q:`What else does Elena do?`,options:[`She trains local health workers.`,`She builds new roads.`,`She teaches English to children.`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-054`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The battery 'lasted for only thirty minutes'; early phones were so expensive that 'only rich business people had them'; there are 'more mobile phones in the world than people'.`,passage:`The first mobile phone call was made in New York in 1973 by an engineer called Martin Cooper. The phone he used weighed more than a kilogram and was about twenty-five centimetres long. Its battery lasted for only thirty minutes, and it took ten hours to charge. Mobile phones became available to buy in the 1980s, but they were very expensive, so only rich business people had them. Today there are more mobile phones in the world than people. A modern smartphone has more computing power than the computers that helped astronauts land on the Moon.`,items:[{q:`How long did the battery of the first mobile phone last?`,options:[`Thirty minutes`,`Ten hours`,`A whole day`],correct:0},{q:`Who had mobile phones in the 1980s?`,options:[`Most teenagers`,`Only astronauts`,`Only rich business people`],correct:2},{q:`What is true today?`,options:[`Mobile phones weigh more than a kilogram.`,`There are more mobile phones than people.`,`Only engineers use mobile phones.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-055`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Plants reduce floods because 'the ground can absorb rainwater'; people near parks 'feel less stressed'; Singapore's artificial trees 'collect rainwater and produce energy'.`,passage:`Many cities around the world are creating more green spaces, such as parks, gardens and even forests on the roofs of buildings. Trees and plants do more than make a city look nicer. They clean the air, give shade on hot days and help to reduce the risk of floods, because the ground can absorb rainwater. Studies also show that people who live near parks feel less stressed and are more likely to exercise. In Singapore, plants grow on the walls of tall buildings, and the city is famous for its huge artificial trees, which collect rainwater and produce energy from the sun.`,items:[{q:`How do green spaces help to reduce floods?`,options:[`They stop it from raining.`,`The ground can absorb rainwater.`,`They make rivers deeper.`],correct:1},{q:`What do studies show about people who live near parks?`,options:[`They feel less stressed.`,`They spend more money.`,`They work longer hours.`],correct:0},{q:`What do the artificial trees in Singapore do?`,options:[`They give food to birds.`,`They make the streets darker.`,`They collect rainwater and produce energy.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-056`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`His manager asked him 'to present a project'; members 'received helpful comments from the others'; after a year 'he won the club's speech competition'.`,passage:`Omar used to be very shy. At school he never spoke in class, and he felt sick when he had to give a presentation. When he started his first job, his manager asked him to present a project to the whole team. Omar was terrified, so he joined a public speaking club that met every Tuesday evening. Each week, members gave a short talk and received helpful comments from the others. At first his hands shook and he forgot what to say. After a year, he won the club's speech competition. Now he enjoys speaking in front of people.`,items:[{q:`Why did Omar join the club?`,options:[`His manager asked him to give a presentation.`,`His friends were members.`,`He wanted to meet new people.`],correct:0},{q:`What happened at each meeting?`,options:[`Members watched films about famous speakers.`,`Members wrote long reports.`,`Members gave short talks and got comments.`],correct:2},{q:`What happened after a year?`,options:[`He left the club.`,`He won the club's speech competition.`,`He became the manager of his team.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-057`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The rock 'hit the Earth near what is now Mexico'; dust 'blocked the sunlight', so plants died; birds are 'the living relatives of some small dinosaurs'.`,passage:`Dinosaurs lived on Earth for about one hundred and sixty million years, much longer than humans have existed. They disappeared around sixty-six million years ago. Most scientists believe that a huge rock from space, about ten kilometres wide, hit the Earth near what is now Mexico. It caused fires, giant waves and clouds of dust that blocked the sunlight for months. Without sunlight, many plants died, and then the animals that ate them died too. However, not all dinosaurs disappeared completely. Scientists now agree that birds are the living relatives of some small dinosaurs.`,items:[{q:`Where did the huge rock hit the Earth?`,options:[`Near what is now Mexico`,`In the middle of Africa`,`Near the North Pole`],correct:0},{q:`Why did many plants die?`,options:[`The rock landed on them.`,`The dust blocked the sunlight.`,`The dinosaurs ate them all.`],correct:1},{q:`What do scientists now agree about birds?`,options:[`They lived before the dinosaurs.`,`They caused the dinosaurs to disappear.`,`They are related to some small dinosaurs.`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-058`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Doctors suggest 'thirty minutes a day, five days a week'; people say a walk helps them 'think clearly'; one tip is to 'get off the bus one stop early'.`,passage:`Walking is one of the easiest ways to stay healthy, and you do not need any special equipment. Doctors say that walking for thirty minutes a day, five days a week, can make your heart stronger and help you control your weight. It is also good for your mind. Many people say that a walk helps them to think clearly and solve problems. If you find it difficult to find time, there are simple changes you can make. For example, you can get off the bus one stop early, use the stairs instead of the lift, or walk while you are talking on the phone.`,items:[{q:`How much walking do doctors suggest?`,options:[`Ten minutes once a week`,`Two hours every day`,`Thirty minutes a day, five days a week`],correct:2},{q:`How do many people say walking helps their mind?`,options:[`It helps them think clearly.`,`It helps them remember names.`,`It helps them sleep during the day.`],correct:0},{q:`Which of these is a tip from the text?`,options:[`Buy expensive walking shoes`,`Get off the bus one stop early`,`Walk only at weekends`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-059`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`Most team members 'helped to carry food, tents and oxygen'; they stayed only fifteen minutes because 'they did not have much oxygen left'; the mountain 'has become crowded'.`,passage:`In 1953, Edmund Hillary from New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay from Nepal became the first people known to reach the top of Mount Everest, the highest mountain in the world. Many climbers had tried before and failed. They were part of a large team, and most members helped to carry food, tents and oxygen up the mountain. Hillary and Norgay stayed at the top for only about fifteen minutes, because they did not have much oxygen left. Today thousands of people have climbed Everest, but it is still very dangerous, and the mountain has become crowded during the climbing season.`,items:[{q:`What did most members of the team do?`,options:[`They helped to carry food, tents and oxygen.`,`They climbed to the top too.`,`They stayed in New Zealand.`],correct:0},{q:`Why did Hillary and Norgay stay at the top for only fifteen minutes?`,options:[`The weather was too bad.`,`They wanted to be home before dark.`,`They did not have much oxygen left.`],correct:2},{q:`What is the situation on Everest today?`,options:[`Nobody climbs it any more.`,`It has become crowded in the climbing season.`,`It is now completely safe.`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-060`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says Tom 'has worked in the same office for six years', that there are 'fewer meetings and nobody interrupts him', and that he misses 'having lunch with his colleagues'.`,passage:`Tom has worked in the same office for six years, but last spring his company decided to let staff work from home two days a week. At first Tom was worried. He thought he might feel lonely and that he wouldn't be able to concentrate with his children around. In fact, the opposite happened. He now finishes his reports faster because there are fewer meetings and nobody interrupts him. He also saves almost two hours a day because he doesn't have to drive into the city. He uses that time to go running in the morning. The only thing he misses is having lunch with his colleagues.`,items:[{q:`How long has Tom worked in the same office?`,options:[`Six years`,`Two years`,`Since last spring`],correct:0},{q:`Why does Tom finish his reports faster now?`,options:[`He works longer hours`,`His children help him`,`He has fewer meetings and interruptions`],correct:2},{q:`What does Tom miss about the office?`,options:[`Driving into the city`,`Eating lunch with his colleagues`,`Going to meetings`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-061`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says Priya 'found maths very difficult', that Mr Okafor 'used games and real-life problems', and that the club meets 'on Tuesdays and Thursdays'.`,passage:`When Priya started secondary school, she found maths very difficult. Her marks were low and she often felt like giving up. Then a new teacher, Mr Okafor, joined the school. Instead of just writing rules on the board, he used games and real-life problems, like planning a class trip on a budget. Priya began to understand why maths was useful. She also started going to a homework club after school on Tuesdays and Thursdays, where older students helped younger ones. By the end of the year, her marks had improved a lot. Now she is thinking about studying engineering at university.`,items:[{q:`How did Priya feel about maths at first?`,options:[`She thought it was easy`,`She found it very hard`,`She was not interested in school`],correct:1},{q:`What did Mr Okafor do differently?`,options:[`He used games and real-life problems`,`He gave more homework`,`He wrote more rules on the board`],correct:0},{q:`When does the homework club meet?`,options:[`Every day after lunch`,`At weekends`,`On Tuesdays and Thursdays`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-062`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says owners 'were afraid that customers wouldn't come', that 'a small market is now held there every Friday', and that the council plans 'to close two more streets next year'.`,passage:`Five years ago, the centre of our town was full of cars and very noisy. Then the local council decided to close the main square to traffic. Many shop owners were against the idea because they were afraid that customers wouldn't come any more. However, the result surprised everyone. Trees and benches were added, and a small market is now held there every Friday. More people walk and cycle into town, and the cafés have put tables outside. Some shops say their sales have actually gone up. The council is now planning to close two more streets next year.`,items:[{q:`Why were shop owners against the plan?`,options:[`They wanted more parking`,`They didn't like trees`,`They thought they would lose customers`],correct:2},{q:`What happens in the square every Friday?`,options:[`Cars are allowed back in`,`There is a small market`,`The cafés close early`],correct:1},{q:`What does the council plan to do next year?`,options:[`Close two more streets to traffic`,`Open the square to cars again`,`Build a new shopping centre`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-063`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says she 'practised in the sea' on Sundays, that 'the cold water was the biggest problem', and that she 'has already signed up for next year's race'.`,passage:`Lucia has always loved swimming, but last year she decided to try something harder: a long-distance swim in the sea. She trained four mornings a week in the local pool before work, and on Sundays she practised in the sea with a group of other swimmers. The cold water was the biggest problem. The first time she went in, she could only stay for ten minutes. Slowly her body got used to it. In September she completed a three-kilometre race along the coast. She didn't win, but she finished in the top half, and she has already signed up for next year's race.`,items:[{q:`Where did Lucia train on Sundays?`,options:[`In the sea`,`In the local pool`,`At a gym`],correct:0},{q:`What was the biggest problem for Lucia?`,options:[`Waking up early`,`Swimming with a group`,`The cold water`],correct:2},{q:`What has Lucia already done?`,options:[`Won a race`,`Signed up for next year's race`,`Stopped swimming in the sea`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-064`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'my grandmother taught me', that she 'told me stories about her childhood' while the dough was rising, and that the daughter 'mostly plays with the dough'.`,passage:`My grandmother taught me to make bread when I was eight years old. Every Saturday morning we used to mix flour, water, salt and yeast in a big bowl in her kitchen. While the dough was rising, she told me stories about her childhood on a farm. I have kept her recipe in a small notebook, and I still bake bread most weekends. Last month my daughter asked me to teach her. She's only six, so she mostly plays with the dough, but she loves it. I hope that one day she will pass the recipe on to her own children.`,items:[{q:`Who taught the writer to make bread?`,options:[`Their mother`,`Their grandmother`,`Their daughter`],correct:1},{q:`What did the grandmother do while the dough was rising?`,options:[`She told stories about her childhood`,`She went to the farm`,`She wrote the recipe in a notebook`],correct:0},{q:`What does the writer's daughter mostly do?`,options:[`Bakes bread on her own`,`Reads the recipe`,`Plays with the dough`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-065`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says his uncle 'gave him an old camera', that each month 'they choose a theme', and that he wants 'to save money to buy a better camera'.`,passage:`Ben started taking photographs when his uncle gave him an old camera for his fifteenth birthday. At first he only took pictures of his friends and his dog. Then he joined a photography club that meets in the library every Wednesday evening. The members go on trips together, and each month they choose a theme, such as 'water' or 'night'. Ben's photo of a bridge in the fog won the club's competition last winter, and it was shown in the town hall for a month. He now wants to save money to buy a better camera, so he is working in a supermarket at weekends.`,items:[{q:`How did Ben get his first camera?`,options:[`He bought it in a supermarket`,`He won it in a competition`,`His uncle gave it to him`],correct:2},{q:`What do the club members choose each month?`,options:[`A place to have dinner`,`A theme for their photos`,`A new member`],correct:1},{q:`Why is Ben working at weekends?`,options:[`To save for a better camera`,`To pay for the photography club`,`To help his uncle`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-066`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the parents wanted 'more space and fresh air', that the children 'missed their friends and the cinema', and that the father is 'much less stressed than before'.`,passage:`The Garcia family moved from a big city to a small village last summer. The parents wanted their two children to grow up with more space and fresh air. Life is very different now. The nearest supermarket is fifteen minutes away by car, and the bus only comes three times a day. At first the children complained because they missed their friends and the cinema. However, they have made new friends at the village school, and they now spend most afternoons outside, building a tree house in the garden. Their father, who works online, says he is much less stressed than before.`,items:[{q:`Why did the family move to the village?`,options:[`To give the children more space and fresh air`,`To be closer to a supermarket`,`Because the father lost his job`],correct:0},{q:`What did the children miss at first?`,options:[`Their tree house`,`The village school`,`Their friends and the cinema`],correct:2},{q:`How does the father feel now?`,options:[`Lonely and bored`,`Less stressed than before`,`Worried about the bus`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-067`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the jacket 'was much cheaper than in the shops', that 'the colour was darker' and 'the sleeves were too long', and that next time the writer will 'try clothes on in a shop first'.`,passage:`Last week I bought a jacket online because it was much cheaper than in the shops. When the parcel arrived, I was disappointed. The colour was darker than in the photo, and the sleeves were too long. I decided to send it back. The website said that returns were free if you sent the item within thirty days, so I printed the label and took the parcel to the post office. Ten days later the money was returned to my bank account. Next time, I think I'll try clothes on in a shop first, even if I have to pay a little more.`,items:[{q:`Why did the writer buy the jacket online?`,options:[`The shops were closed`,`It was cheaper than in the shops`,`It was a present`],correct:1},{q:`What was wrong with the jacket?`,options:[`The colour and the size of the sleeves`,`It was broken`,`It arrived late`],correct:0},{q:`What will the writer probably do next time?`,options:[`Buy only online`,`Never buy a jacket again`,`Try clothes on in a shop first`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-068`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'more than half' read news on social media, that forty percent 'had shared a story without checking if it was true', and that lessons teach students 'to look at the source and to compare different websites'.`,passage:`A recent survey asked five hundred teenagers how they get their news. More than half said they mostly read news on social media, and only one in ten said they watched the news on television. Many said that short videos were easier to understand than long articles. However, the survey also found a problem: about forty percent admitted that they had shared a story without checking if it was true. Teachers in several schools have now started lessons to help students recognise false news. They teach them to look at the source and to compare different websites before believing a story.`,items:[{q:`Where do most of the teenagers get their news?`,options:[`On television`,`In newspapers`,`On social media`],correct:2},{q:`What problem did the survey find?`,options:[`Teenagers never read the news`,`Many teenagers shared stories without checking them`,`Short videos were too difficult`],correct:1},{q:`What do the new lessons teach students?`,options:[`To check the source and compare websites`,`To make their own videos`,`To stop using social media`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-069`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'eighty students took part, together with some parents and teachers', that 'all the plastic was taken to a recycling centre', and that the council 'promised to put more bins'.`,passage:`Every spring, students at Hillside School organise a clean-up day at the river near their town. This year more than eighty students took part, together with some parents and teachers. They wore gloves and collected rubbish from the river banks for four hours. They found plastic bottles, bags, an old bicycle and even a shopping trolley. All the plastic was taken to a recycling centre. After the clean-up, the students made posters asking people not to throw rubbish near the water. The town council was so impressed that it has promised to put more bins along the river path.`,items:[{q:`Who took part in the clean-up day?`,options:[`Students, parents and teachers`,`Only teachers`,`Workers from the council`],correct:0},{q:`What happened to the plastic they collected?`,options:[`It was thrown in the river`,`It was sold`,`It was taken to a recycling centre`],correct:2},{q:`What has the council promised to do?`,options:[`Close the river path`,`Put more bins along the river path`,`Organise the next clean-up`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-070`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'it's difficult to sleep during the day', that he feels proud 'when someone who was very ill is able to go home', and that he is 'going to start a course' in children's care.`,passage:`Marco works as a nurse in a busy hospital. He usually works twelve-hour shifts, and some weeks he has to work at night. He says the night shifts are the hardest, because it's difficult to sleep during the day when everyone else is awake. To help, he has bought thick curtains and he turns off his phone. Although the job is tiring, Marco loves it. He enjoys talking to patients and he feels proud when someone who was very ill is able to go home. Next year he is going to start a course to become a specialist in children's care.`,items:[{q:`Why are night shifts the hardest for Marco?`,options:[`He doesn't like the patients at night`,`It's hard to sleep during the day`,`The hospital is very quiet`],correct:1},{q:`What makes Marco feel proud?`,options:[`When a very ill patient can go home`,`When he works twelve hours`,`When he buys new curtains`],correct:0},{q:`What is Marco going to do next year?`,options:[`Stop working at night`,`Change hospitals`,`Start a course in children's care`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-071`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says she 'took a year off to travel and work', 'taught English to young children in Vietnam', and that 'the year taught her more than any classroom could'.`,passage:`When Aisha finished school, she didn't go straight to university. Instead, she took a year off to travel and work. She spent three months working on a farm in New Zealand, where she picked fruit and looked after sheep. Then she taught English to young children in Vietnam for four months. She says the year taught her more than any classroom could. She learned to manage her own money, to solve problems on her own and to talk to people from very different cultures. Now she is in her second year of a degree in international relations, and she feels sure she chose the right subject.`,items:[{q:`What did Aisha do after finishing school?`,options:[`She went straight to university`,`She started teaching in her home town`,`She took a year off to travel and work`],correct:2},{q:`What did she do in Vietnam?`,options:[`Picked fruit`,`Taught English to children`,`Looked after sheep`],correct:1},{q:`What does Aisha say about her year away?`,options:[`It taught her more than a classroom could`,`It was a waste of time`,`It made her want to leave university`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-072`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says you 'unlock it with your phone', that 'some bikes have been damaged or left in the wrong places', and that electric bikes 'will be useful for people who live on the hills'.`,passage:`Our city has had a bike-sharing scheme for two years. You download an app, find a bike at one of the two hundred stations and unlock it with your phone. The first thirty minutes are free, and after that you pay a small amount for every extra half hour. The scheme has been very popular with students and office workers. However, there have been some problems. Some bikes have been damaged or left in the wrong places, and at busy times it can be hard to find one. The city is now adding electric bikes, which will be useful for people who live on the hills.`,items:[{q:`How do you unlock a bike?`,options:[`With your phone`,`With a key from the station`,`By paying cash`],correct:0},{q:`What is one problem with the scheme?`,options:[`It is too expensive for students`,`There are no stations in the city centre`,`Some bikes are damaged or left in the wrong places`],correct:2},{q:`Why will the electric bikes be useful?`,options:[`They are free all day`,`They will help people who live on the hills`,`They are faster than buses`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-073`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says his friend showed him the rules 'during a long train journey', that the club 'has players of all ages', and that 'you can see how nervous or confident they are'.`,passage:`Sam had never played chess until his friend showed him the rules during a long train journey. He lost every game that day, but he was hooked. Back home, he started playing online in the evenings and watching videos to learn new strategies. After a few months he joined a chess club at the community centre. The club has players of all ages, from children of seven to a man who is over ninety. Sam says that playing against real people is very different from playing on a screen, because you can see how nervous or confident they are. He has just played in his first tournament.`,items:[{q:`Where did Sam learn the rules of chess?`,options:[`At the community centre`,`On a train journey`,`From online videos`],correct:1},{q:`Who plays at the chess club?`,options:[`People of all ages`,`Only children`,`Only experienced players`],correct:0},{q:`Why does Sam prefer playing real people?`,options:[`The games are shorter`,`He always wins`,`He can see how they feel`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-074`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'her grandparents opened it', that Elena was worried when 'a large supermarket opened nearby', and that people can 'order birthday cakes' on the website.`,passage:`Elena's family has run a small bakery in the same street for over forty years. Her grandparents opened it, and her parents took it over in the 1990s. Two years ago, a large supermarket opened nearby, and Elena was worried that customers would stop coming. To compete, the family decided to make changes. They started selling sandwiches and coffee, and they began delivering bread to local cafés every morning. They also created a website where people can order birthday cakes. Business is now better than ever, and Elena, who studied business at college, is planning to open a second shop next year.`,items:[{q:`Who opened the bakery?`,options:[`Elena's parents`,`Elena`,`Elena's grandparents`],correct:2},{q:`Why was Elena worried two years ago?`,options:[`The bakery was too old`,`A large supermarket opened nearby`,`Her parents wanted to close it`],correct:1},{q:`What can people order on the website?`,options:[`Birthday cakes`,`Coffee`,`Sandwiches for delivery`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-075`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says he started a garden 'on his balcony', that plants died 'because he gave them too much water', and that neighbours 'have started growing plants, and they exchange seeds and tips'.`,passage:`Jonas started a vegetable garden on his balcony last year, although he lives on the sixth floor of an apartment building. He uses large pots and grows tomatoes, lettuce, herbs and strawberries. At the beginning, many of his plants died because he gave them too much water. He read some books, asked his neighbours for advice and learned from his mistakes. This summer he hasn't bought any salad from the shop for three months. He has also given extra tomatoes to the old lady next door. Now several other people in the building have started growing plants, and they exchange seeds and tips.`,items:[{q:`Where is Jonas's garden?`,options:[`On his balcony`,`In a park`,`In his neighbour's garden`],correct:0},{q:`Why did many plants die at the beginning?`,options:[`It was too cold`,`He forgot to water them`,`He gave them too much water`],correct:2},{q:`What have other people in the building started doing?`,options:[`Buying salad for Jonas`,`Growing plants and sharing seeds`,`Selling tomatoes`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-076`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'they used to walk to school together', that they have 'a video call every Sunday evening', and that 'they are closer now than when they lived in the same house'.`,passage:`When Nadia's brother left home to study in another country, she was only twelve. She missed him a lot at first, because they used to walk to school together every day. Now they have a video call every Sunday evening. He shows her his tiny student room and tells her about his classes, and she tells him about her friends and her basketball team. Last summer, Nadia and her parents flew out to visit him. He showed them around the city and took them to his favourite restaurant. Nadia says they are closer now than when they lived in the same house.`,items:[{q:`What did Nadia and her brother use to do together?`,options:[`Play basketball`,`Walk to school`,`Cook dinner`],correct:1},{q:`When do they have a video call?`,options:[`Every Sunday evening`,`Every morning`,`Once a month`],correct:0},{q:`What does Nadia say about their relationship now?`,options:[`They don't talk much any more`,`They argue more often`,`They are closer than before`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-077`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the station 'is run completely by volunteers', that the teenagers 'interview people in the town about their jobs', and that it won awards 'for the way it brings the community together'.`,passage:`Our local radio station is run completely by volunteers. It started ten years ago in a small room above a shop, and now it broadcasts twenty-four hours a day. There are programmes about music, local news, sport and cooking. One of the most popular shows is presented by two teenagers who interview people in the town about their jobs. Anyone can offer to help: some people present programmes, while others answer the phones or look after the website. The station doesn't make any money, but it has won two awards for the way it brings the community together.`,items:[{q:`Who works at the radio station?`,options:[`Professional journalists`,`Shop workers`,`Volunteers`],correct:2},{q:`What do the two teenagers do on their show?`,options:[`Play only music`,`Interview people about their jobs`,`Read the local news`],correct:1},{q:`Why has the station won awards?`,options:[`It brings the community together`,`It makes a lot of money`,`It has the best music`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-078`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says she 'started writing it down', that she now 'plans meals for the week before she goes shopping', and that the family is 'spending about twenty percent less on food'.`,passage:`Rosa had never thought about how much food her family threw away until she started writing it down. In one week, they threw away bread, half a bag of salad, some yoghurt and a lot of leftover pasta. She decided to change some habits. Now she plans meals for the week before she goes shopping, and she only buys what is on her list. Leftovers are put in boxes and eaten for lunch the next day. Old bread is turned into breadcrumbs or soup. After two months, Rosa's family is spending about twenty percent less on food, and their bin is much emptier.`,items:[{q:`How did Rosa find out how much food her family wasted?`,options:[`She wrote it down for a week`,`She read about it online`,`Her children told her`],correct:0},{q:`What does Rosa do before she goes shopping now?`,options:[`She empties the bin`,`She makes soup`,`She plans meals for the week`],correct:2},{q:`What is one result of the changes?`,options:[`They eat out more often`,`The family spends less on food`,`They buy more bread`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-079`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says he worked 'on Saturdays', that he 'learned to work quickly' and 'to stay polite', and that he now 'manages a hotel'.`,passage:`Kevin got his first job at sixteen, working in a fast-food restaurant on Saturdays. He had to wear a uniform, arrive at seven in the morning and clean the kitchen before customers came in. The work was hard and the pay was low, but he learned a lot. He learned to work quickly when the restaurant was busy, and to stay polite even when customers were rude. His manager, a patient woman called Joy, often gave him advice. Kevin is now twenty-five and manages a hotel. He says that many of the skills he uses every day were first learned in that small restaurant.`,items:[{q:`When did Kevin work at the restaurant?`,options:[`Every evening`,`On Saturdays`,`During the summer only`],correct:1},{q:`What did Kevin learn at the restaurant?`,options:[`To work fast and stay polite`,`To cook difficult dishes`,`To manage a hotel`],correct:0},{q:`What does Kevin do now?`,options:[`He owns a restaurant`,`He is still working with Joy`,`He manages a hotel`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-080`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says students 'can use them during breaks and at lunchtime', that 'phones were distracting students' and some were cheating, and that the college will 'provide more computers in the library'.`,passage:`Greenfield College has decided to ban mobile phones in classrooms from next September. Students will still be allowed to bring their phones to school, but they must keep them in their bags during lessons. They can use them during breaks and at lunchtime. The head teacher explained that phones were distracting students and that some had been using them to cheat in tests. Many parents support the decision. However, some students are unhappy, because they often use their phones to look up information or check their timetable. The college has promised to provide more computers in the library so that students can still do research.`,items:[{q:`When can students use their phones?`,options:[`During lessons`,`Only after school`,`During breaks and at lunchtime`],correct:2},{q:`Why did the college make this decision?`,options:[`Phones were too expensive`,`Phones were distracting students and some were cheating`,`Parents asked for more computers`],correct:1},{q:`What has the college promised?`,options:[`More computers in the library`,`A new timetable`,`Free phones for students`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-081`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'the streets are narrow and very steep', the tram 'was crowded but great fun', and the writer would 'love to go back in spring'.`,passage:`I visited Lisbon for the first time last October with two friends. We stayed in a small apartment in an old part of the city, where the streets are narrow and very steep. We walked everywhere, although our legs were tired by the end of each day. We also took one of the famous yellow trams, which was crowded but great fun. The food was excellent, especially the fish and the little custard cakes. On our last evening we watched the sunset from a viewpoint high above the river. I'd love to go back in spring, when it's warm but not too hot.`,items:[{q:`What were the streets like where they stayed?`,options:[`Narrow and steep`,`Wide and flat`,`Busy with cars`],correct:0},{q:`How did the writer describe the tram?`,options:[`Empty and slow`,`Expensive and boring`,`Crowded but fun`],correct:2},{q:`When would the writer like to go back?`,options:[`In October`,`In spring`,`In summer`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-082`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the coach told her 'she could become a great passer', that 'in her second season, she became the team's captain', and that 'they lost by just two points'.`,passage:`Hana joined her school's basketball team when she was thirteen, even though she was one of the shortest players. The coach told her that height wasn't everything and that she could become a great passer. She practised every day after school, and she watched professional games to see how the best players moved. In her second season, she became the team's captain. Last month the team reached the final of the regional competition for the first time. They lost by just two points, but Hana says she has never been prouder of her teammates. They have already started training for next season.`,items:[{q:`What did the coach tell Hana?`,options:[`She was too short to play`,`She could become a great passer`,`She should try another sport`],correct:1},{q:`What happened in her second season?`,options:[`She became captain`,`She left the team`,`She changed schools`],correct:0},{q:`What was the result of the final?`,options:[`The team won easily`,`The final was cancelled`,`The team lost by two points`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-083`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'an empty car park is turned into a food market', that 'most dishes cost less than eight pounds', and that 'it's best to arrive before six o'clock'.`,passage:`Street food markets have become very popular in many cities. In my town, an empty car park is turned into a food market every Thursday evening. There are about thirty stalls, selling dishes from all over the world: Mexican tacos, Indian curries, Japanese noodles and much more. Most dishes cost less than eight pounds, so it's cheaper than a restaurant. People sit at long tables and often talk to strangers. There is usually live music too. The only problem is that it can be very crowded, so if you want to avoid long queues, it's best to arrive before six o'clock.`,items:[{q:`Where is the food market held?`,options:[`In a shopping centre`,`In a park`,`In an empty car park`],correct:2},{q:`Why is the market cheaper than a restaurant?`,options:[`The food is free`,`Most dishes cost less than eight pounds`,`You bring your own food`],correct:1},{q:`What does the writer suggest?`,options:[`Arriving before six to avoid queues`,`Going on Saturdays`,`Sitting alone`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-084`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'her family couldn't afford lessons', that 'she practised for twenty minutes every evening', and that she plays 'with three friends from work'.`,passage:`Olivia started learning to play the guitar at the age of forty. She had always wanted to play, but when she was young her family couldn't afford lessons. After her children grew up, she finally bought a second-hand guitar and found a teacher online. At first her fingers hurt and she couldn't play a single song correctly. She practised for twenty minutes every evening, even when she was tired. After a year, she could play several simple songs. Now she plays in a small band with three friends from work, and they have performed twice at a local café.`,items:[{q:`Why didn't Olivia learn the guitar when she was young?`,options:[`Her family couldn't pay for lessons`,`She wasn't interested in music`,`She had no time`],correct:0},{q:`How often did Olivia practise?`,options:[`Once a week for an hour`,`Only at weekends`,`Every evening for twenty minutes`],correct:2},{q:`Who does Olivia play with now?`,options:[`Her children`,`Three friends from work`,`Her online teacher`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-085`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the family meets 'at my aunt's house by the lake', that 'my grandfather always makes a speech', and that 'my cousin is getting married'.`,passage:`Every year in August, my whole family gets together at my aunt's house by the lake. There are about twenty-five of us, including cousins, grandparents and new babies. Everyone brings food, and we eat together at a long table in the garden. The children swim in the lake and play football, while the adults talk and play cards. On the last night, my grandfather always makes a speech. He reminds us how important it is to stay in touch, even though we live in different countries now. This year my cousin is getting married, so the party will be even bigger.`,items:[{q:`Where does the family meet every year?`,options:[`At the grandparents' house`,`At the writer's aunt's house`,`In a hotel`],correct:1},{q:`What does the grandfather do on the last night?`,options:[`He makes a speech`,`He cooks for everyone`,`He plays football`],correct:0},{q:`Why will the party be bigger this year?`,options:[`More babies have been born`,`The aunt has a new house`,`A cousin is getting married`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-086`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says she 'used the money to buy a coat', that 'making new clothes uses a lot of water and energy', and that 'she sometimes buys too much because the prices are so low'.`,passage:`More and more people are buying second-hand clothes. There are now many websites and apps where you can sell clothes you don't wear any more and buy things from other people. My sister uses one of these apps all the time. Last month she sold six dresses and used the money to buy a coat that was almost new. She says it's good for the environment, because making new clothes uses a lot of water and energy. She also enjoys finding unusual things that nobody else has. The only problem, she says, is that she sometimes buys too much because the prices are so low.`,items:[{q:`What did the writer's sister buy with the money from the dresses?`,options:[`Six new dresses`,`A new phone`,`A coat`],correct:2},{q:`Why does she think second-hand clothes are good for the environment?`,options:[`Second-hand clothes last longer`,`Making new clothes uses a lot of water and energy`,`Shops create too much rubbish`],correct:1},{q:`What problem does she mention?`,options:[`She sometimes buys too much`,`The clothes are often damaged`,`The app is difficult to use`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-087`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says you can listen 'while you are cooking, travelling to work or doing exercise', that his podcast is 'about the history of his city', and that the museum 'has asked him to work with them'.`,passage:`Podcasts have become one of the most popular ways to learn new things. You can listen to them while you are cooking, travelling to work or doing exercise. My friend Daniel listens to at least one podcast every day. His favourites are about history and science, but he also enjoys comedy shows. Last year he decided to make his own podcast about the history of his city. He records it in his bedroom with a cheap microphone and interviews older people about how the city used to be. He now has over two thousand regular listeners, and the local museum has asked him to work with them.`,items:[{q:`When can people listen to podcasts, according to the text?`,options:[`While cooking, travelling or exercising`,`Only at home in the evening`,`Only in a museum`],correct:0},{q:`What is Daniel's podcast about?`,options:[`Comedy`,`Science`,`The history of his city`],correct:2},{q:`What has the local museum done?`,options:[`Bought him a new microphone`,`Asked Daniel to work with them`,`Made its own podcast`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-088`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the quarry was 'full of broken glass and rubbish', that 'some rare birds ... have come back', and that visitors 'are asked to stay on the paths and to keep their dogs on a lead'.`,passage:`Ten years ago, the old quarry outside our village was a dirty, empty place full of broken glass and rubbish. Then a group of local volunteers decided to turn it into a nature reserve. They cleaned the area, planted hundreds of trees and created a small lake. Today the reserve is home to many kinds of birds, frogs and insects. Some rare birds that hadn't been seen in the area for years have come back. Schools often bring children there to learn about nature. Visitors are asked to stay on the paths and to keep their dogs on a lead so that the animals are not disturbed.`,items:[{q:`What was the quarry like ten years ago?`,options:[`A popular park`,`Dirty and full of rubbish`,`A busy farm`],correct:1},{q:`What has happened to some rare birds?`,options:[`They have come back to the area`,`They have all disappeared`,`They were moved to another reserve`],correct:0},{q:`What are visitors asked to do?`,options:[`Feed the birds`,`Bring their own trees`,`Stay on the paths and keep dogs on a lead`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-089`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says putting out fires 'is only a small part of the job', that 'the most important quality ... is staying calm', and that 'now there are four women in her team'.`,passage:`Fatima works as a firefighter in a large city. Most people think firefighters spend all their time putting out fires, but that is only a small part of the job. Fatima's team also helps people after car accidents, rescues people who are trapped in lifts and visits schools to teach children about fire safety. When there are no emergencies, they clean their equipment, do exercise and practise rescues. Fatima says the most important quality for a firefighter is staying calm. She was the first woman to join her station, but now there are four women in her team.`,items:[{q:`What does the text say about putting out fires?`,options:[`It is the only thing firefighters do`,`Fatima's team never does it`,`It is only a small part of the job`],correct:2},{q:`What does Fatima think is the most important quality?`,options:[`Being very strong`,`Staying calm`,`Being able to drive fast`],correct:1},{q:`How many women are in Fatima's team now?`,options:[`Four`,`One`,`Two`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-090`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the emails were written 'half in English and half in French', that Lucas 'was shy at first', and that 'in October it's our turn to visit France'.`,passage:`Last year our school started a language exchange with a school in France. Each student was given a partner of the same age. For six months we wrote emails to each other, half in English and half in French, so we could both practise. In May the French students came to stay with our families for a week. They came to lessons with us, and we took them to see the sights. My partner, Lucas, was shy at first, but by the end of the week we were laughing like old friends. In October it's our turn to visit France, and I can't wait.`,items:[{q:`How did the partners write their emails?`,options:[`Half in English and half in French`,`Only in English`,`Only in French`],correct:0},{q:`How was Lucas at first?`,options:[`Rude`,`Very talkative`,`Shy`],correct:2},{q:`What will happen in October?`,options:[`Lucas will come back`,`The writer's class will visit France`,`The exchange will end`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-091`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'the view was amazing', that 'the lift broke down last winter', and that 'it's also quite windy on the balcony'.`,passage:`When I moved to the city, I chose a flat on the twelfth floor of a tall building because the view was amazing. I can see the river, the old cathedral and, on clear days, the mountains far away. However, living so high has some disadvantages. When the lift broke down last winter, I had to climb the stairs for a whole week, carrying my shopping. It's also quite windy on the balcony, so I can't grow many plants. Still, I wouldn't change it. Every evening, when the city lights come on, I sit by the window with a cup of tea and enjoy the view.`,items:[{q:`Why did the writer choose the flat?`,options:[`Because it was cheap`,`Because of the view`,`Because it was near work`],correct:1},{q:`What happened last winter?`,options:[`The lift broke down`,`The balcony was damaged`,`The writer moved out`],correct:0},{q:`Why can't the writer grow many plants?`,options:[`There is no balcony`,`The writer doesn't like plants`,`The balcony is very windy`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-092`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says it happens 'every Saturday at nine o'clock', that 'it isn't a race', and that the father 'has made so many new friends'.`,passage:`Running has become very popular in my town since a free weekly event called 'Park Run' started two years ago. Every Saturday at nine o'clock, people meet in the main park and run five kilometres together. It isn't a race, so you can run, jog or even walk. Volunteers record everyone's time, and the results are sent by email afterwards. Some people come with their dogs or push their babies in special buggies. My father, who is sixty-eight, has done it more than eighty times. He says it is the best part of his week because he has made so many new friends.`,items:[{q:`When does Park Run happen?`,options:[`Every evening`,`Once a month`,`Every Saturday at nine o'clock`],correct:2},{q:`Why can people walk instead of run?`,options:[`Running is not allowed`,`It isn't a race`,`The park is too small`],correct:1},{q:`Why does the writer's father enjoy it so much?`,options:[`He has made many new friends`,`He always wins`,`He gets a prize each time`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-093`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says he decided 'after watching a documentary about farming', that the parents were worried 'he wouldn't get enough protein', and that the family eats vegetarian meals 'three times a week'.`,passage:`My brother Leo decided to become vegetarian last year after watching a documentary about farming. At first our parents were worried that he wouldn't get enough protein, so they asked a doctor for advice. The doctor explained that beans, eggs, nuts and cheese are all good sources of protein. Now the whole family eats vegetarian meals three times a week, and we have discovered lots of new dishes. My favourite is a spicy bean soup that Leo makes. My dad still eats meat, but he says he doesn't miss it as much as he expected on the vegetarian days.`,items:[{q:`Why did Leo become vegetarian?`,options:[`After watching a documentary about farming`,`Because a doctor told him to`,`Because he didn't like the taste of meat`],correct:0},{q:`What were the parents worried about?`,options:[`That Leo would cook too much`,`That food would cost more`,`That Leo wouldn't get enough protein`],correct:2},{q:`How often does the family eat vegetarian meals?`,options:[`Every day`,`Three times a week`,`Only at weekends`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-094`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'she found a box of cards in her grandparents' attic', that the beach 'doesn't exist any more', and that she plans 'to show part of her collection at the local library'.`,passage:`Mia has collected old postcards since she was ten. It all started when she found a box of cards in her grandparents' attic. Some of them were more than a hundred years old and had messages written by people who were on holiday. Now she has over two thousand postcards from sixty different countries. She buys them at markets and online, and friends often send her cards when they travel. Her favourite is a card from 1925 showing a beach that doesn't exist any more because the sea has covered it. She is planning to show part of her collection at the local library next spring.`,items:[{q:`How did Mia's hobby start?`,options:[`A friend sent her a card`,`She found old postcards in her grandparents' attic`,`She bought a card at a market`],correct:1},{q:`Why is the beach on her favourite card special?`,options:[`It doesn't exist any more`,`It is in sixty countries`,`It belongs to her grandparents`],correct:0},{q:`What is Mia planning to do?`,options:[`Sell her collection`,`Stop collecting`,`Show some of her cards at the library`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-095`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the parents were told about 'a quiet dinner', that 'my mum started crying', and that they 'danced to the song they had danced to at their wedding'.`,passage:`My parents have been married for thirty years, and last month my sister and I organised a surprise party for them. It wasn't easy to keep it a secret. We told them we were taking them out for a quiet dinner, but instead we took them to a hall where sixty friends and relatives were waiting. Some of the guests had travelled from other countries. My mum started crying when she saw everyone, and my dad was speechless for the first time in his life. We showed old photos on a big screen, and at the end of the evening my parents danced to the song they had danced to at their wedding.`,items:[{q:`What did the parents think they were going to do?`,options:[`Go to a wedding`,`Travel abroad`,`Have a quiet dinner`],correct:2},{q:`How did the mother react?`,options:[`She left the party`,`She started crying`,`She made a speech`],correct:1},{q:`What did the parents do at the end of the evening?`,options:[`They danced to their wedding song`,`They watched a film`,`They went home early`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-096`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says shops have been closing 'because more people shop online', that 'ten stamps give you a free gift', and that 'three empty shops have reopened'.`,passage:`Small shops in our high street have been closing for years because more people shop online. Last year, a group of shop owners decided to work together to bring customers back. They created a loyalty card that can be used in all twenty shops. Every time you spend ten pounds, you get a stamp, and ten stamps give you a free gift. They also started a late-night shopping evening on the first Friday of every month, with music and free snacks. So far, the plan seems to be working. Three empty shops have reopened, including a bookshop and a shop that repairs bicycles.`,items:[{q:`Why have small shops been closing?`,options:[`More people shop online`,`The rent is too high`,`There aren't enough customers in the town`],correct:0},{q:`What do you get with ten stamps?`,options:[`Ten pounds`,`A free snack`,`A free gift`],correct:2},{q:`What is one sign that the plan is working?`,options:[`All the shops now close later`,`Three empty shops have reopened`,`The shops have moved online`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-097`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says it 'shows old classic films on Monday evenings', that on Sundays 'the lights stay on a little and the sound is quieter', and that visitors 'went up by fifteen percent'.`,passage:`Many people now watch films and series on streaming services instead of going to the cinema. However, our town's small cinema has found a way to survive. It shows old classic films on Monday evenings, and the tickets are half price. On Sundays it has special morning shows for families with young children, where the lights stay on a little and the sound is quieter. It also organises film quizzes in its café. The owner says that people still want to go out and share the experience with others. Last year, the number of visitors actually went up by fifteen percent.`,items:[{q:`What does the cinema show on Monday evenings?`,options:[`New action films`,`Old classic films`,`Children's films`],correct:1},{q:`What is special about the Sunday morning shows?`,options:[`The lights stay on a little and the sound is quieter`,`Tickets are free`,`They are only for adults`],correct:0},{q:`What happened to the number of visitors last year?`,options:[`It went down`,`It stayed the same`,`It went up`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-098`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'it has stopped selling plastic bags completely', that for each bottle 'you get a small amount of money off your shopping', and that 'staff stood near the machines to help them'.`,passage:`Many families in our area have started using reusable shopping bags, and the local supermarket has decided to go further. Since January, it has stopped selling plastic bags completely. Customers can bring their own bags or buy strong cloth bags at the checkout. The supermarket has also put special machines near the entrance where people can return empty bottles. For each bottle, you get a small amount of money off your shopping. Some older customers found the changes confusing at first, so staff stood near the machines to help them. The manager says the shop now uses about a million fewer plastic bags a year.`,items:[{q:`What has the supermarket stopped doing?`,options:[`Selling bottles`,`Opening in January`,`Selling plastic bags`],correct:2},{q:`What do people get for returning a bottle?`,options:[`A free cloth bag`,`Money off their shopping`,`A new bottle`],correct:1},{q:`How did staff help older customers?`,options:[`They stood near the machines to help`,`They carried their shopping`,`They gave them free bags`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-099`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says he 'lives in a small town in Spain', that he starts at two 'because of the time difference', and that the company 'is going to fly all its workers to Toronto'.`,passage:`Carlos is a software developer who works for a company in Canada, but he lives in a small town in Spain. Thanks to the internet, he can work from anywhere. Because of the time difference, he usually starts work at two in the afternoon and finishes at ten at night. This means his mornings are free. He often goes for a walk in the hills or does the shopping before the shops get busy. The hardest part is that he has never met most of his colleagues in person. Next spring, however, the company is going to fly all its workers to Toronto for a week-long meeting.`,items:[{q:`Where does Carlos live?`,options:[`In a small town in Spain`,`In Toronto`,`In a big city in Canada`],correct:0},{q:`Why does Carlos start work in the afternoon?`,options:[`Because he goes walking in the morning`,`Because his company is closed in the morning`,`Because of the time difference`],correct:2},{q:`What is going to happen next spring?`,options:[`Carlos will move to Canada`,`Carlos will meet his colleagues in Toronto`,`Carlos will change jobs`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-100`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'all his friends had already passed', that he 'got too nervous during tests', and that he 'stopped telling his friends when his test was'.`,passage:`When Jake failed his driving test for the second time, he felt embarrassed. All his friends had already passed, and they often joked about it. His instructor told him that he was a good driver but that he got too nervous during tests. She suggested that he try some breathing exercises and take a few extra lessons in the area where the test was held. Jake also stopped telling his friends when his test was, so he felt less pressure. On his third attempt he passed with only two small mistakes. He says he learned that failing isn't the end of the world.`,items:[{q:`Why did Jake feel embarrassed?`,options:[`He had an accident`,`His friends had all passed`,`His instructor was angry`],correct:1},{q:`What did the instructor say was Jake's problem?`,options:[`He got too nervous during tests`,`He drove too fast`,`He didn't know the rules`],correct:0},{q:`What did Jake stop doing?`,options:[`Taking lessons`,`Doing breathing exercises`,`Telling his friends the date of his test`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-101`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'there are more bikes in the city than people', that family bikes are 'for carrying small children or shopping', and that visitors 'should be careful not to walk in the bike lanes'.`,passage:`Amsterdam is famous for its bicycles. In fact, there are more bikes in the city than people. Children learn to ride when they are very young, and many families have special bikes with a large box at the front for carrying small children or shopping. There are bike lanes on almost every street, and in many places cyclists have their own traffic lights. Visitors are often surprised by how fast the cyclists go, so they should be careful not to walk in the bike lanes. If you want to try cycling there yourself, you can easily rent a bike from one of the many shops in the centre.`,items:[{q:`What does the text say about the number of bikes in Amsterdam?`,options:[`There are very few bikes`,`Bikes are only for children`,`There are more bikes than people`],correct:2},{q:`What are the special family bikes used for?`,options:[`Racing`,`Carrying small children or shopping`,`Delivering post`],correct:1},{q:`What should visitors be careful about?`,options:[`Not walking in the bike lanes`,`Not riding too slowly`,`Not using the traffic lights`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-102`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the party 'was held at an indoor climbing centre', that 'you have to think carefully about where to put your hands and feet', and that 'she was scared at the top' but 'the view made it worth it'.`,passage:`Rachel had never been interested in sport until she tried rock climbing at a friend's birthday party. The party was held at an indoor climbing centre, and after an hour on the walls, she was completely hooked. She started going twice a week and soon became much stronger. Climbing, she says, is good for both the body and the mind, because you have to think carefully about where to put your hands and feet. Last summer she went climbing outdoors for the first time, in the mountains with an experienced guide. Although she was scared at the top, she says the view made it worth it.`,items:[{q:`Where did Rachel first try climbing?`,options:[`At an indoor climbing centre`,`In the mountains`,`At school`],correct:0},{q:`Why does Rachel think climbing is good for the mind?`,options:[`It is very relaxing`,`You meet a lot of people`,`You have to think carefully about your moves`],correct:2},{q:`How did Rachel feel at the top of the mountain?`,options:[`Bored`,`Scared, but the view was worth it`,`Too tired to enjoy it`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-103`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the writer 'thought Japanese food was just sushi', that in Japan people eat 'lots of small dishes in one meal', and that Kenji 'is teaching me to use chopsticks properly'.`,passage:`My flatmate Kenji is from Japan, and he has taught me a lot about Japanese food. Before I met him, I thought Japanese food was just sushi. Now I know that there are many other dishes, like hot noodle soups, rice bowls with egg and meat, and pancakes filled with cabbage. Kenji says that in Japan people usually eat lots of small dishes in one meal instead of one big plate. Every Sunday we cook dinner together, and he is teaching me to use chopsticks properly. I'm still very slow, and I drop rice everywhere, but Kenji says I'm getting better.`,items:[{q:`What did the writer think about Japanese food before meeting Kenji?`,options:[`It was too spicy`,`It was only sushi`,`It was very expensive`],correct:1},{q:`How do people in Japan usually eat, according to Kenji?`,options:[`Many small dishes in one meal`,`One big plate`,`Only noodles and rice`],correct:0},{q:`What is Kenji teaching the writer?`,options:[`To speak Japanese`,`To make sushi`,`To use chopsticks properly`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-104`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the group 'was started ... by a retired teacher', that they 'knitted over a hundred hats and gloves' for a shelter, and that 'it's about friendship and not feeling alone'.`,passage:`Every Thursday evening, a group of about fifteen people meet in a café in my town to knit and chat. The group was started three years ago by a retired teacher called Margaret. Some members are experienced and can make complicated sweaters, while others are complete beginners who are learning to make their first scarf. The youngest member is fourteen and the oldest is eighty-seven. Last winter, the group knitted over a hundred hats and gloves and gave them to a shelter for homeless people. Margaret says the group isn't really about knitting; it's about friendship and not feeling alone.`,items:[{q:`Who started the knitting group?`,options:[`The café owner`,`A fourteen-year-old student`,`A retired teacher`],correct:2},{q:`What did the group do last winter?`,options:[`Opened their own café`,`Knitted hats and gloves for a homeless shelter`,`Sold sweaters`],correct:1},{q:`According to Margaret, what is the group really about?`,options:[`Friendship and not feeling alone`,`Making money`,`Winning competitions`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-105`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'teachers sometimes called me by her name, which made me angry', that at fifteen they changed their hair and 'chose different subjects', and that they 'phone each other almost every day'.`,passage:`When my twin sister and I were children, people often couldn't tell us apart. We wore the same clothes, had the same haircut and were in the same class at school. Teachers sometimes called me by her name, which made me angry. When we were fifteen, we decided to change things. I cut my hair short and she dyed hers red. We also chose different subjects: she studied art, while I chose science. Now we live in different cities and have very different lives. However, we still phone each other almost every day, and she is still my best friend.`,items:[{q:`Why did the writer get angry as a child?`,options:[`Teachers called her by her sister's name`,`Her sister copied her homework`,`She had to wear the same clothes`],correct:0},{q:`What did the sisters do at fifteen?`,options:[`Moved to different schools`,`Stopped talking to each other`,`Changed their hair and chose different subjects`],correct:2},{q:`How often do the sisters talk now?`,options:[`Once a month`,`Almost every day`,`Only on their birthday`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-106`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says her list had 'a new winter coat and some running shoes', that 'she was shocked by how much she had spent', and that she will 'wait twenty-four hours before buying anything expensive'.`,passage:`Before Black Friday last year, my friend Sophie made a list of things she really needed: a new winter coat and some running shoes. However, when the day came, she saw so many special offers that she bought much more than she had planned. She came home with three sweaters, a coffee machine and a pair of boots she has never worn. When her bank statement arrived, she was shocked by how much she had spent. This year she has decided to try a different approach. She will only buy items on her list, and she will wait twenty-four hours before buying anything expensive.`,items:[{q:`What did Sophie plan to buy last year?`,options:[`A coffee machine`,`A winter coat and running shoes`,`Three sweaters`],correct:1},{q:`How did Sophie feel when she saw her bank statement?`,options:[`Shocked`,`Happy`,`Relaxed`],correct:0},{q:`What will Sophie do this year before buying something expensive?`,options:[`Ask a friend`,`Buy it immediately`,`Wait twenty-four hours`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-107`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'the only way to get the news was from the newspaper or the radio', that 'everyone went to the house of the only family that had one', and that 'he still prefers holding a real newspaper'.`,passage:`When my grandfather was young, the only way to get the news was from the newspaper or the radio. The family listened to the evening news together every day after dinner. When television arrived in their village, everyone went to the house of the only family that had one, to watch important events. Today my grandfather has a tablet, and I showed him how to read the news online. At first he found it confusing, but now he reads three different news websites every morning. He says he still prefers holding a real newspaper, though, especially on Sundays.`,items:[{q:`How did the grandfather's family get the news when he was young?`,options:[`From television`,`From the internet`,`From the newspaper or the radio`],correct:2},{q:`What happened when television arrived in the village?`,options:[`Every family bought one`,`People went to the only house that had one`,`The radio stopped working`],correct:1},{q:`What does the grandfather still prefer?`,options:[`Holding a real newspaper`,`Watching the news on television`,`Listening to the radio`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-108`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says people were told 'not to water their gardens or wash their cars', that crops were lost 'because the ground was too dry', and that the town plans 'to collect rainwater in large tanks'.`,passage:`Last summer was the hottest our region has ever had. For weeks there was no rain, and the river near our town was lower than anyone could remember. The local council asked people to save water. They were told not to water their gardens or wash their cars, and to take short showers instead of baths. Many farmers lost part of their crops because the ground was too dry. Scientists say that summers like this will probably become more common because of climate change. As a result, the town is now planning to collect rainwater in large tanks so that it can be used in dry periods.`,items:[{q:`What were people asked not to do?`,options:[`Water their gardens or wash their cars`,`Take showers`,`Drink tap water`],correct:0},{q:`Why did farmers lose part of their crops?`,options:[`There was too much rain`,`The river flooded`,`The ground was too dry`],correct:2},{q:`What is the town planning to do?`,options:[`Build a new river`,`Collect rainwater in large tanks`,`Stop farmers from growing crops`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-109`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'her day starts at half past seven', that she enjoys 'seeing a child suddenly understand something', and that 'the hardest part is the amount of paperwork'.`,passage:`Anna has been a primary school teacher for twelve years. Her day starts at half past seven, when she arrives at school to prepare her lessons. She teaches a class of twenty-eight eight-year-olds, and she says that no two days are ever the same. Some days the children are full of energy, and other days they are tired and quiet. The part of the job she enjoys most is seeing a child suddenly understand something that was difficult for them. The hardest part is the amount of paperwork, which she often has to finish at home in the evening.`,items:[{q:`What time does Anna arrive at school?`,options:[`Eight o'clock`,`Half past seven`,`Half past eight`],correct:1},{q:`What does Anna enjoy most about her job?`,options:[`Seeing a child suddenly understand something`,`Doing paperwork`,`Finishing early`],correct:0},{q:`What is the hardest part of the job for her?`,options:[`The children's energy`,`Preparing lessons`,`The paperwork`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-110`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says it is open 'twenty-four hours a day during exam periods', that students 'can book a group room online up to a week in advance', and that the top floor has 'a view of the whole city'.`,passage:`Our university has opened a new library that is open twenty-four hours a day during exam periods. It has quiet rooms for people who want to study alone and group rooms where students can work on projects together. There are also comfortable sofas, a café on the ground floor and hundreds of power sockets for laptops. Students can book a group room online up to a week in advance. The most popular place is the top floor, which has large windows with a view of the whole city. Some students say they now spend more time in the library than in their own rooms.`,items:[{q:`When is the library open all day and night?`,options:[`Every weekend`,`All year round`,`During exam periods`],correct:2},{q:`How can students book a group room?`,options:[`At the café`,`Online, up to a week before`,`By phoning the library on the day`],correct:1},{q:`Why is the top floor so popular?`,options:[`It has a view of the whole city`,`It has the café`,`It is the quietest floor`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-111`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says he goes 'with his father', that 'he couldn't eat' because he was nervous, and that being a fan is 'about being part of something'.`,passage:`My cousin Pablo has been a football fan all his life. He has supported the same team since he was five, even though they have never won a major trophy. He goes to every home game with his father, and they always sit in the same seats. When the team plays away, he watches the match at a bar with other fans. Last season the team nearly went down to a lower league, and Pablo was so nervous before the last game that he couldn't eat. Luckily, they won two-nil. He says that being a fan isn't about winning; it's about being part of something.`,items:[{q:`Who does Pablo go to home games with?`,options:[`His father`,`His cousin`,`His friends from school`],correct:0},{q:`What happened before the last game of last season?`,options:[`Pablo missed the game`,`The team won a major trophy`,`Pablo was too nervous to eat`],correct:2},{q:`What does being a fan mean to Pablo?`,options:[`Winning trophies`,`Being part of something`,`Going to away games`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-112`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says she 'broke her leg' and 'lives alone', that neighbours 'water their plants and feed their cats', and that before the accident 'most of us didn't even know each other's names'.`,passage:`Two years ago, my neighbour Mrs Brown fell and broke her leg. Since she lives alone, a few people in our street started taking turns to bring her meals and do her shopping. Even after her leg got better, we continued to meet. Now about ten households have a group chat where we ask for help, lend tools and share news. When someone goes on holiday, others water their plants and feed their cats. Last month we organised a street party, and everyone brought food to share. It's strange to think that before Mrs Brown's accident, most of us didn't even know each other's names.`,items:[{q:`Why did people start helping Mrs Brown?`,options:[`She asked for money`,`She broke her leg and lives alone`,`She was going on holiday`],correct:1},{q:`What do neighbours do when someone goes on holiday?`,options:[`Water their plants and feed their cats`,`Clean their house`,`Look after their children`],correct:0},{q:`What was true before Mrs Brown's accident?`,options:[`Everyone had a group chat`,`There was a street party every month`,`Most neighbours didn't know each other's names`],correct:2}]},{id:`b1-rm-113`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says 'he often gets up before sunrise', that the otters 'appeared for less than a minute', and that his fox photo 'was chosen for the cover of a nature magazine'.`,passage:`Wildlife photography has been Ibrahim's hobby for almost ten years. He often gets up before sunrise and drives to a forest or a lake, where he waits quietly for hours. He says patience is the most important skill, because animals don't appear when you want them to. Once he waited six hours in the rain to photograph a family of otters, and they appeared for less than a minute. He always follows one rule: never disturb the animals. Last year one of his photos of a fox in the snow was chosen for the cover of a nature magazine.`,items:[{q:`When does Ibrahim often go out to take photos?`,options:[`In the afternoon`,`Late at night`,`Before sunrise`],correct:2},{q:`What happened when he waited for the otters?`,options:[`They never came`,`They appeared for less than a minute`,`He took hundreds of photos`],correct:1},{q:`What happened to his photo of a fox?`,options:[`It was used on a magazine cover`,`It won a prize for animals`,`He sold it to a forest centre`],correct:0}]},{id:`b1-rm-114`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says the writer 'bought a lamp for my bedroom', that families 'gave part of their money to the local children's hospital', and that unsold things were 'taken to a charity shop'.`,passage:`Last weekend our street had a huge garage sale. About thirty families put tables outside their houses and sold things they no longer needed: old toys, books, clothes, furniture and kitchen equipment. Prices were very low, and people were happy to bargain. I sold my old bicycle and some board games, and I bought a lamp for my bedroom for just two pounds. Most families gave part of their money to the local children's hospital. Anything that wasn't sold was taken to a charity shop at the end of the day, so very little was thrown away.`,items:[{q:`What did the writer buy?`,options:[`A lamp`,`A bicycle`,`Some board games`],correct:0},{q:`Where did part of the money go?`,options:[`To the council`,`To a new playground`,`To the local children's hospital`],correct:2},{q:`What happened to the things that weren't sold?`,options:[`They were thrown away`,`They were taken to a charity shop`,`They were kept for next year`],correct:1}]},{id:`b1-rm-115`,type:`reading-mc`,level:`B1`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and answer the questions.`,explanation:`The text says her channel is about 'cooking cheap, healthy meals for students', that in the first year 'she had fewer than a hundred followers and almost gave up', and that one video 'was shared by a famous chef'.`,passage:`Many young people dream of becoming online video stars, but my friend Chloe knows how much work it involves. She started a channel about cooking cheap, healthy meals for students two years ago. She spends about twenty hours a week planning, filming and editing her videos. For the first year, she had fewer than a hundred followers and almost gave up. Then one of her videos, about making five meals with just ten pounds, was shared by a famous chef. Within a week she had fifty thousand new followers. She now earns some money from her channel, but she still works part-time in a bookshop.`,items:[{q:`What is Chloe's channel about?`,options:[`Famous chefs`,`Cheap, healthy meals for students`,`Books`],correct:1},{q:`What happened during her first year?`,options:[`She had very few followers and almost gave up`,`She became famous`,`She stopped making videos`],correct:0},{q:`What helped Chloe get many new followers?`,options:[`She started working in a bookshop`,`She spent more money on her videos`,`A famous chef shared one of her videos`],correct:2}]}]};export{e as default};